      ],
      "lineAnnotations": {
        "0": {"synopsis":"Hardware reset entry. At reset every page register is 0, so these bytes run at $0000; the\ncode switches to interrupt mode 1 and jumps on into this segment's page-3 copy at\nRESET_PAGE3 with\nA = 4. EXOS 0 (reset) jumps here too when bit 7 of C is set.","comment":"No interrupts until the RST 30h code is in RAM"},
        "1": {"comment":"Interrupts go to $0038"},
        "3": {"comment":"Value for the wait-mode port BFh"},
        "5": {"comment":"Continue in page 3 (segment 0 is in every page)"},
        "8": {"synopsis":"Device chain pointer in the extension-ROM header's place: the page-1 address ($5780) of\nVIDEO's size byte, the first built-in pseudo-descriptor. LINK_ROM_DEVS reads it as ($4008)\nwith this segment in page 1."},
        "10": {"synopsis":"Entry addresses of EXOS functions 17-34 (capture channel ... read date), one word each,\nindexed by (code - 17) * 2 in KRN_DISPATCH. Codes 31, 33 and 34 enter EXOS_SET_TIME,\nEXOS_SET_DATE and EXOS_READ_DATE, a run of INC A instructions ending in SEG1_SERVICE, where\ncode 32 (read time) enters."},
        "46": {"synopsis":"Reset continued in page 3: sets the wait mode, then looks for a test ROM in segment 4. A\nsegment whose first 8 bytes are \"TEST_ROM\" is entered at its $0008 (in page 0); otherwise\nthe reset goes on at NO_TEST_ROM.","comment":"Wait mode (port BFh) = 4"},
        "48": {"comment":"Page 0 = segment 4"},
        "52": {"comment":"Its first 8 bytes"},
        "55": {"comment":"against TEST_ROM_ID"},
        "60": {"synopsis":"Loop: compares one header byte; a difference leaves for NO_TEST_ROM."},
        "64": {"comment":"Not a test ROM?"},
        "68": {"comment":"Enter the test ROM at $0008"},
        "69": {"synopsis":"No test ROM: puts the system segment FFh into pages 1 and 2 and sets the stack below $B217.\nA zero RST_ADDR means a cold start (COLD_START). Otherwise, when the 59 bytes at $B217 still\nmatch the pager code (PAGER_CALL), the RAM is taken to be intact and the start goes to\nWARM_START; any difference makes it a cold start.","comment":"System segment"},
        "71": {"comment":"into page 2"},
        "73": {"comment":"and page 1"},
        "75": {"comment":"RST_ADDR: the warm reset address"},
        "78": {"comment":"Zero? (cold start)"},
        "80": {"comment":"Stack just below the pager code"},
        "86": {"comment":"Compare the pager copy at $B217"},
        "89": {"comment":"59 bytes"},
        "91": {"synopsis":"Loop: compares one byte of the pager copy; a difference makes the start a cold one."},
        "93": {"comment":"RAM corrupted: cold start"},
        "99": {"comment":"Pager intact: warm start"},
        "102": {"synopsis":"Cold start:\n1. Black border; tests the system segment FFh (in page 1) with TEST_RAM_SEG; a fault stops\n   in FATAL_FLASH.\n2. Puts segment FFh in page 2, sets STACK_LIMIT to $AC3B, initialises VIDEO (its entry +24\n   at VID_INIT) and stores $730B at $B902.\n3. Adds the 16384 words of the 32K EXOS ROM (segments 0 and 1 in pages 0 and 1); a correct\n   ROM sums to 0.\n4. Copies BOOT_TEXT (34 bytes) or, after a bad sum, CHECKSUM_TEXT (23 bytes) to $BEBC; a bad\n   sum then stops in FATAL_FLASH.","comment":"Page 2 = segment FEh while FFh is tested"},
        "106": {"comment":"Border black"},
        "109": {"comment":"Test segment FFh (in page 1)"},
        "112": {"comment":"Faulty: stop"},
        "114": {"comment":"A = FFh"},
        "115": {"comment":"Page 2 = the system segment"},
        "117": {"comment":"STACK_LIMIT = $AC3B"},
        "123": {"comment":"VIDEO initialise (entry +24)"},
        "132": {"comment":"Checksum HL = 0, BC = address 0"},
        "137": {"comment":"Page 0 = ROM segment 0"},
        "140": {"comment":"Page 1 = ROM segment 1"},
        "142": {"synopsis":"Loop: adds the next little-endian word to HL until BC reaches $8000.","comment":"Next word to DE"},
        "148": {"comment":"Add to the sum"},
        "149": {"comment":"Past $7FFF?"},
        "153": {"comment":"Sum zero? (Z = ROM good)"},
        "155": {"comment":"Error text, 23 bytes"},
        "161": {"comment":"Bad sum?"},
        "163": {"comment":"Good: the 34-byte text"},
        "168": {"synopsis":"Copies the chosen text to $BEBC; a bad sum (NZ) then falls into FATAL_FLASH.","comment":"Text goes to $BEBC"},
        "173": {"comment":"Sum was good?"},
        "175": {"synopsis":"Endless loop that steps the border colour: the stop for a faulty system segment, a bad ROM\nchecksum or no usable RAM.","comment":"Next border colour, forever"},
        "180": {"synopsis":"Tests every segment from FEh down to 08h that can be written (in page 1) with TEST_RAM_SEG.\nThe number of each good one is stored downward from $ABCF, below an $FF end marker at $ABD0.\nIn the alternate set E counts the good segments + 1 and D the faulty ones; a faulty segment\nalso stores $49 at $B90B.","comment":"$B90B = 0: no faulty segment yet"},
        "186": {"comment":"$FF ends the segment list at $ABD0"},
        "190": {"comment":"$BF9A = the list's top"},
        "194": {"comment":"Alternate DE: D = faulty, E = good + 1"},
        "197": {"synopsis":"Loop over segments FEh-08h: pages the segment into page 1; when its first byte cannot be\ninverted it is not RAM and is skipped.","comment":"Next lower segment"},
        "198": {"comment":"into page 1"},
        "204": {"comment":"Invert the first byte"},
        "207": {"comment":"Did it change? (RAM)"},
        "210": {"comment":"Test the whole segment"},
        "215": {"comment":"Faulty: $B90B = $49"},
        "222": {"synopsis":"A good segment: undoes the faulty count of TEST_RAM_SEG, counts it and stores its number in\nthe list.","comment":"Not faulty after all"},
        "223": {"comment":"One more good segment"},
        "225": {"comment":"Next list entry, down"},
        "226": {"comment":"Store the segment number"},
        "230": {"synopsis":"Loop end: stops after segment 08h."},
        "231": {"comment":"Segment 08h done?"},
        "235": {"comment":"$BFA3 = good + 1, $BFA4 = faulty"},
        "239": {"comment":"No good segment?"},
        "242": {"comment":"E = good - 1 (the lowest is taken)"},
        "243": {"comment":"$BF9F = segments left free"},
        "247": {"comment":"$BFA2 + 1"},
        "251": {"comment":"The lowest good segment"},
        "252": {"comment":"is the null segment: USR_P0"},
        "255": {"comment":"$BF9C = its list entry"},
        "258": {"comment":"First ROM list entry: segment 0"},
        "262": {"comment":"Scan segments F0h, E0h ... 10h"},
        "264": {"synopsis":"Loop: checks the first segment of each 16-segment block (F0h down to 10h) for an \"EXOS_ROM\"\nheader (CHECK_ROM_SEG with NZ: no mirror test)."},
        "267": {"comment":"Next block down"},
        "271": {"comment":"Then segments 7, 6, 5 and 4"},
        "273": {"synopsis":"Loop: checks segments 7 down to 4 for an \"EXOS_ROM\" header (CHECK_ROM_SEG with Z set by CP\nA: a copy of a lower segment is skipped)."},
        "274": {"comment":"Z: test for mirrors"},
        "278": {"comment":"Segment 4 done?"},
        "282": {"comment":"Last entry: ROM segment 1"},
        "287": {"comment":"$BF97 and $BF95 = the list's end"},
        "293": {"comment":"$BFE8 = 2"},
        "298": {"comment":"$BF7E = $A7"},
        "303": {"comment":"$BFD5 = $0F"},
        "308": {"comment":"$BFE7 = $FF (NZ for the jump below)"},
        "313": {"synopsis":"Warm start (C = $10 for EXOS 0); falls into START_COMMON.","comment":"EXOS 0 flags: bit 4"},
        "315": {"synopsis":"Common start, also entered with C already set ($0ADF, C = $20): puts the null segment\n(USR_P0) into page 0, copies the pager to $B217 and the RST 30h code to $0030, takes\nRST_ADDR to the stack and clears it, and marks EXOS as running ($BF79 = $80). A cold start\n(NZ from $0134) then goes to SEG1_SERVICE with A = 6; a warm one enters the RST 30h code\npast its\nfunction fetch ($0042) with function 0 (reset) and the old RST_ADDR as its return address."},
        "316": {"comment":"Page 0 = the null segment"},
        "321": {"comment":"Pager code to $B217"},
        "332": {"comment":"RST 30h code to $0030-$005A"},
        "339": {"comment":"The warm reset address"},
        "342": {"comment":"becomes the return address"},
        "343": {"comment":"RST_ADDR = 0 (BC = 0 after LDIR)"},
        "347": {"comment":"$BF79 = $80: EXOS running"},
        "352": {"comment":"Cold start: segment 1 service 6 via SEG1_SERVICE"},
        "358": {"comment":"EXOS 0 (reset) with C from above"},
        "359": {"comment":"Into the RST 30h code at $0042"},
        "362": {"synopsis":"Writes one hex digit of A to (HL).\nEntry: A = value; the digit shown is the high nibble.\nExit: A has its nibbles swapped (a second call shows the low nibble and restores A).\nChanges: AF.","comment":"Swap the nibbles"},
        "367": {"comment":"Digit to ASCII 0-9, A-F"},
        "378": {"synopsis":"Text copied to $BEBC at a cold start when the ROM checksum is good (34 bytes): \"EXOS 2.1\",\nspaces, \"TESTING\", spaces, then \"ERROR\" in letters with bit 7 set. TEST_RAM_SEG writes the\nsegment under test into it at $BED5."},
        "412": {"synopsis":"Text copied to $BEBC when the ROM checksum is wrong (23 bytes, every letter with bit 7 set):\n\"INTERNAL CHECKSUM ERROR\"."},
        "435": {"synopsis":"Puts segment A into page 1 and records it in the ROM list when its first 8 bytes are\n\"EXOS_ROM\".\nEntry: A = segment; HL (alternate set) = the next list entry; Z = skip the segment when its\nfirst 64 bytes equal a lower segment's (down to 4), NZ = no such test.\nExit: a recorded segment is stored at (HL) and HL moves 4 bytes down.\nChanges: AF; page 0 when testing for mirrors.","comment":"Page 1 = the segment"},
        "437": {"comment":"Keep the caller's Z flag"},
        "439": {"comment":"Its first 8 bytes"},
        "442": {"comment":"against EXOS_ROM_ID"},
        "447": {"synopsis":"Loop: compares one header byte; a difference skips the segment."},
        "451": {"comment":"Not an EXOS ROM?"},
        "455": {"comment":"Caller's flags back"},
        "456": {"comment":"NZ: record without the mirror test"},
        "460": {"synopsis":"Loop: tries the next lower segment (down to 4) as the one A may mirror; below 4 the segment\nis unique and is recorded."},
        "461": {"comment":"Next lower segment"},
        "462": {"comment":"Below 4? (no copy found)"},
        "466": {"comment":"Page 0 = the lower segment"},
        "468": {"comment":"Compare $0000 with $4000"},
        "473": {"comment":"64 bytes"},
        "475": {"synopsis":"Loop: compares one of the 64 bytes; a difference tries the next lower segment."},
        "477": {"comment":"Different: try the next"},
        "483": {"synopsis":"Not a ROM, or a copy of a lower segment: returns without recording it."},
        "486": {"synopsis":"No copy found: drops the saved flags and records the segment."},
        "487": {"synopsis":"Back to the alternate set and records segment A."},
        "488": {"synopsis":"Stores A at (HL) and moves HL 4 bytes down: one 4-byte ROM list entry, segment number at the\ntop.","comment":"Segment number"},
        "489": {"comment":"Next entry, 4 bytes down"},
        "494": {"synopsis":"The 8-byte header that marks an extension ROM segment, compared by CHECK_ROM_SEG:\n\"EXOS_ROM\"."},
        "502": {"synopsis":"The 8-byte header of a test ROM in segment 4, compared by RESET_PAGE3: \"TEST_ROM\"."},
        "510": {"synopsis":"Tests the 16K segment in page 1.\n1. Counts it in D as faulty (the caller takes the count back on success) and writes its\n   number as two hex digits into the boot text at $BED5; $B909 = the segment.\n2. Fills the segment with the inverted bytes of $C000-$FFFF, checking each write.\n3. Walks back down adding each byte to its ROM byte + 1 (0 when the inverted value held) and\n   clears it.\nExit: Z = good, the segment now all zeros; NZ = faulty.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL, D.","comment":"Count as faulty for now"},
        "511": {"comment":"The segment being tested"},
        "513": {"comment":"Shown in the boot text"},
        "523": {"comment":"$B909 = the segment"},
        "526": {"comment":"Segment from $4000, pattern from $C000"},
        "532": {"synopsis":"Loop: writes a ROM byte, checks it, then leaves its inverse in the RAM byte, up to $FFFF in\nthe ROM.","comment":"Pattern byte"},
        "534": {"comment":"Held? (else NZ = faulty)"},
        "536": {"comment":"Leave the inverse"},
        "540": {"comment":"Past $FFFF?"},
        "544": {"synopsis":"Loop: from the top down, checks that each RAM byte still holds the inverse of its ROM byte\nand clears it; ends with Z below $4000."},
        "546": {"comment":"Below $4000? (Z: all good)"},
        "549": {"comment":"ROM + inverse + 1 = 0"},
        "552": {"comment":"Clear the byte"},
        "553": {"comment":"Faulty?"},
        "556": {"synopsis":"Low bytes of the system-segment variables at $BFxx that RESET_SYSTEM clears (after $BFF3),\nended by $00."},
        "570": {"synopsis":"EXOS 0 (reset), the work for flag bits 5-0 of C (called from $066F with A = C * 4).\n1. Calls RELEASE_SYS_SEG until it returns Z.\n2. Clears PORTB5 and the variables listed in RESET_ZERO_LIST, writes 0 to ports B5h and B6h,\n   sets $BFCE, $BFDA and $BFD9 to $FF.\n3. The first time ($BF78 = 0), offers RAM to the extension ROMs (ROM_RAM_ALLOC).\n4. With bit 5 of C set (A bit 7) it empties the device chain and links the devices of every\n   ROM again (LINK_ROMS_LP); then RESET_DEVICES.\nFalls into RESET_DEVICES.","comment":"Keep the flags (S = bit 5 of C)"},
        "571": {"synopsis":"Loop: calls RELEASE_SYS_SEG until it returns Z (the byte at ($BF9A) is $FF)."},
        "576": {"comment":"Variables to clear"},
        "580": {"comment":"PORTB5 first"},
        "583": {"synopsis":"Loop: clears $BFxx for each low byte in the list; the $00 ends it."},
        "584": {"comment":"Next variable's low byte"},
        "586": {"comment":"End of list?"},
        "589": {"comment":"Port B5h = 0 (keyboard row, REM, tape sound)"},
        "591": {"comment":"Port B6h = 0 (printer data)"},
        "594": {"comment":"$BFCE, $BFDA, $BFD9 = $FF"},
        "603": {"comment":"$BF78: RAM given to the ROMs yet?"},
        "608": {"comment":"No: offer it now"},
        "612": {"comment":"Bit 5 of C clear: keep the devices"},
        "615": {"comment":"Device area back to its top"},
        "621": {"comment":"Empty device chain (segment 0)"},
        "625": {"comment":"Walk the ROM list from its end"},
        "628": {"synopsis":"Loop: links the devices of ROM segment A (LINK_ROM_DEVS), starting with segment 0, then each\nlist entry upward until the entry holding 0."},
        "629": {"comment":"Link this ROM's devices"},
        "633": {"comment":"Next entry up"},
        "638": {"comment":"Segment 0's entry? (end)"},
        "641": {"synopsis":"Second half of RESET_SYSTEM:\n1. $BFB4 = $BFBD - ($BF93); $BF8D = ($BF93) with bit 6 set.\n2. Links the address ($BF93) in segment FFh at the head of the list headed at $BFBA\n   (LINK_NODE),\n   copies the 8 bytes $02D5-$02DC below it and keeps the address below them in $BF91 (bits\n   7-6 clear).\n3. Sets the default device to TAPE (SET_DEFDEV_AT, EXOS 19's body).\n4. Enables the 1 Hz and video interrupts (port B4h = $14, kept in $BFC5).\n5. Calls the initialise entry (+24) of every device in the chain, then sends action 8\n   (initialise) to the extensions (CALL_EXTENSIONS).\nFalls into ERR_NOFN.","comment":"Bottom of the device area"},
        "651": {"comment":"$BFB4 = the area's size"},
        "655": {"comment":"$BF8D = it with bit 6 set"},
        "663": {"comment":"Page 1 = the system segment"},
        "666": {"comment":"Link it at the head at $BFBA"},
        "671": {"comment":"Copy $02D5-$02DC below it"},
        "682": {"comment":"$BF91 = the address below them"},
        "685": {"comment":"Default device: TAPE"},
        "691": {"comment":"Enable the 1 Hz and video interrupts"},
        "698": {"comment":"Walk the device chain"},
        "701": {"synopsis":"Loop: calls each device's initialise entry (+24) and restores page 1 to the device's\nsegment, until the chain ends."},
        "706": {"comment":"Entry +24: initialise"},
        "717": {"comment":"Action 8 (initialise) to extensions"},
        "722": {"synopsis":"Returns error $E7 (.NOFN): the device entry of every function a device does not support, and\nthe end of RESET_DEVICES.","comment":"Error $E7 (.NOFN)"},
        "725": {"synopsis":"The 16-byte header's lower 8 bytes for the channel chain's first node, which RESET_DEVICES\ncopies just below the link it makes at $BFBA: free gap 0, total size $000C, size kept in one\nsegment $000C, key 0 (no channel) and flags 1."},
        "733": {"synopsis":"The default device's name: length 4, \"TAPE\". RESET_DEVICES passes it to SET_DEFDEV_AT (EXOS\n19's\nbody)."},
        "738": {"synopsis":"Sends action 7 (RAM allocation) to every ROM in the list, once ($BF78 is incremented first).\nFor each ROM that answers C = 0 it allocates DE bytes: with B bit 0 set in the system\nsegment below ($BF95), if that stays at or above $83E8; otherwise (or then) with B bit 1 set\nin a device segment (ALLOC_DEV_RAM, from the top). The area's address (page-1 form for bit\n1) and segment go into the 3 bytes below the ROM's list entry; a ROM whose request fails\ngets its entry overwritten with $FF.","comment":"$BF78 = 1: done"},
        "739": {"comment":"From the null segment's list entry"},
        "743": {"synopsis":"Loop: takes the next ROM list entry; segment 0 ends the walk."},
        "744": {"synopsis":"Moves to the next ROM list entry, 4 bytes down; the entry holding 0 ends the walk."},
        "747": {"synopsis":"Last step down to the next ROM list entry, after the 3 RAM bytes were stored."},
        "748": {"comment":"ROM segment"},
        "749": {"comment":"Segment 0? (end of list)"},
        "752": {"comment":"Action 7: RAM allocation"},
        "754": {"comment":"Through the ROM's entry at $C00A"},
        "761": {"comment":"C nonzero: no RAM wanted"},
        "764": {"comment":"A = the type flags, BC = the size"},
        "767": {"comment":"Carry = bit 0: system segment"},
        "771": {"comment":"Lowest system RAM so far"},
        "775": {"comment":"Room below it?"},
        "779": {"comment":"Not below $83E8"},
        "787": {"comment":"Taken: new lowest address"},
        "791": {"comment":"Carry = bit 1: page-1 form wanted"},
        "792": {"comment":"Segment FFh"},
        "796": {"synopsis":"No room in the system segment, or not asked for: with B bit 1 set, allocates the bytes in a\ndevice segment from the top; otherwise the ROM fails."},
        "797": {"comment":"Carry = bit 1: device segment"},
        "799": {"comment":"Not asked for: fail"},
        "801": {"comment":"Take BC bytes from a device segment"},
        "806": {"comment":"Lower the segment's free top"},
        "813": {"comment":"Flag bit 0: holds ROM RAM"},
        "815": {"comment":"Page 1's segment, but POP AF at $0332 overwrites it"},
        "819": {"synopsis":"Converts the address to page-1 form when bit 1 of B was set.","comment":"Bit 1 clear: keep the page-2 form"},
        "821": {"comment":"$8xxx to $4xxx"},
        "825": {"synopsis":"Stores the RAM segment and address (high, low) in the 3 bytes below the ROM's list entry and\ngoes on with the next ROM. On the device-segment path A is the request byte popped at $0332\n(B rotated), not the segment read at $032F, so that byte is what gets stored as the segment.\nNo ROM Klive ships takes that path: EXDOS 1.3 asks for $07D4 bytes with B = 1 and gets\nsegment $FF (observed)."},
        "827": {"comment":"Below the ROM's segment number:"},
        "828": {"comment":"RAM segment"},
        "830": {"comment":"address high, then low"},
        "835": {"synopsis":"The request cannot be met: overwrites the ROM's list entry with $FF and goes on with the\nnext ROM."},
        "837": {"comment":"Mark the ROM unusable"},
        "841": {"synopsis":"Finds BC bytes in the chain of device segments, adding a new segment when none has room.\nEntry: BC = size; A = 0: from the bottom, skipping segments with flag bit 1; A not 0: from\nthe top.\nExit: A = 0, the segment in page 1, its header at $4000 (IX); HL = the free area's bottom\n($4001) with A = 0 on entry, the new top (($4003) - BC) otherwise; or A = $F7 (.NORAM), NZ.\nThe chain starts at $BF99; in each segment $4000 holds the next one (0 = none), $4001 the\nbottom and $4003 the top of its free area, $4005 flags. A jump back to $0355 skips the DD\nprefix of LD IX,$4000 and runs LD HL,$4000: the next link.","comment":"Z = from the bottom (A = 0)"},
        "843": {"comment":"More than $3FF9 bytes?"},
        "849": {"comment":"First link: $BF99"},
        "852": {"comment":"$0355 (jumped to) is LD HL,$4000"},
        "856": {"comment":"Next device segment"},
        "857": {"comment":"None left: add one"},
        "860": {"comment":"Page it in"},
        "862": {"comment":"Free space = top - bottom"},
        "871": {"comment":"Less than BC? Try the next"},
        "877": {"comment":"From the top: any segment will do"},
        "879": {"comment":"From the bottom: flag bit 1 set?"},
        "884": {"comment":"Yes: try the next"},
        "888": {"synopsis":"No segment has room: allocates a segment (EXOS 24's body, no shared segment), links it at\n(HL) and writes its empty header: next 0, bottom $4007, top $8000, flags 0.","comment":"NZ for EXOS_ALLOC_SEG: no shared segment"},
        "892": {"comment":"Allocate a segment"},
        "899": {"comment":"None: error .NORAM"},
        "901": {"comment":"Link it from the previous one"},
        "902": {"comment":"Page it in"},
        "906": {"comment":"Header at $4000 (D = $40)"},
        "908": {"comment":"No next segment"},
        "910": {"comment":"Free bottom = $4007"},
        "916": {"comment":"Free top = $8000"},
        "920": {"comment":"Flags = 0"},
        "921": {"synopsis":"A segment with room is in page 1: returns the bottom of its free area, or the top less BC."},
        "922": {"comment":"From the bottom: HL = the bottom"},
        "926": {"comment":"From the top: HL = top - size"},
        "933": {"synopsis":"Returns error $F7 (.NORAM) with NZ."},
        "934": {"comment":"Error $F7 (.NORAM)"},
        "938": {"synopsis":"Runs at $B217 in the system segment (copied there by START_COMMON). Calls the routine at HL\nwith segment A in page 3, then puts segment 0 back into page 3.\nEntry: A = segment; HL = routine; A' = the routine's A.\nExit: A and F from the routine (through A'); page 3 = segment 0.","comment":"Page 3 = segment A"},
        "940": {"comment":"Routine's A"},
        "941": {"comment":"Call (HL) through $B225"},
        "945": {"comment":"Page 3 = segment 0 again"},
        "950": {"synopsis":"Runs at $B223: jumps to HL with segment A in page 3.","comment":"Page 3 = segment A"},
        "952": {"synopsis":"Runs at $B225: jumps to HL; called to make a CALL (HL)."},
        "953": {"synopsis":"Runs at $B226: the end of every EXOS call and interrupt. Puts H into page 3, takes L for\npage 2 and the caller's HL from $BF7C, then jumps to $0051 in the null segment\n(KERNEL_RETURN), which sets page 2 and returns.","comment":"Page 3 = the caller's"},
        "956": {"comment":"Page 2 value, set at $0051"},
        "957": {"comment":"Caller's HL"},
        "960": {"comment":"Out through the null segment's code"},
        "963": {"synopsis":"A text copied to $B230-$B251 with the pager code but never run: \"Written by:\" and initials\nin letters with bit 7 set."},
        "997": {"synopsis":"Runs at $0030 in the null segment (copied there by START_COMMON): the RST 30h entry of every\nEXOS call. Saves A at $005A, takes the function byte after the RST from the caller's code\nand steps the return address past it, then goes on at ENTER_KERNEL with carry clear (a call,\nnot an interrupt).","comment":"No interrupts while EXOS enters"},
        "998": {"comment":"Caller's A to $005A"},
        "1001": {"comment":"Carry clear: an EXOS call"},
        "1002": {"comment":"HL = the return address"},
        "1005": {"synopsis":"Runs at $0038 in the null segment: the IM 1 interrupt entry. Saves AF and goes on at\nENTER_KERNEL with carry set (an interrupt)."},
        "1006": {"comment":"Carry set: an interrupt"},
        "1009": {"synopsis":"Runs at $003C: a jump to the software interrupt routine, whose address is the operand at\n$003D/$003E (0 at reset). KRN_EXIT reaches it through the JR it writes at $0057.","comment":"Operand = software interrupt address"},
        "1012": {"synopsis":"Runs at $003F: fetches the function byte and stores it at $0059. START_COMMON enters at\n$0042 with the code already in A.","comment":"Function byte after the RST"},
        "1013": {"comment":"Return past it"},
        "1015": {"comment":"($0042) Function code to $0059"},
        "1018": {"synopsis":"Runs at $0045: keeps the caller's page 3 at $0055, puts segment 0 into page 3 and jumps on\ninto it at KERNEL_ENTRY.","comment":"Caller's page 3"},
        "1020": {"comment":"kept at $0055"},
        "1023": {"comment":"Page 3 = segment 0 (this one)"},
        "1027": {"comment":"On in the ROM"},
        "1030": {"synopsis":"Runs at $0051: the null segment's tail of every exit. Sets page 2 from A; the bytes after it\nare written by the kernel before each exit: $0054 the status for LD A,n, $0055 OR A (or POP\nAF after an interrupt), $0056 EI (or OR A, or NOP), $0057 RET (or JR to SOFT_IRQ_JP, with\nthe $E3 at $0058 as its offset).","comment":"Page 2 = the caller's"},
        "1032": {"comment":"($0053) A = status, operand written"},
        "1034": {"comment":"($0055) OR A / POP AF, written"},
        "1035": {"comment":"($0056) EI / OR A / NOP, written"},
        "1036": {"comment":"($0057) RET, or JR to $003C"},
        "1037": {"comment":"($0058) Offset of that JR: -29"},
        "1038": {"synopsis":"Two bytes of the null-segment code used as variables: $0059 the function code of the current\ncall, $005A the caller's A."},
        "1040": {"synopsis":"The kernel's side of every EXOS call and interrupt (from ENTER_KERNEL, carry set for an\ninterrupt).\n1. Keeps the caller's page 2 at $0054, puts the system segment into page 2 and keeps HL at\n   $BF7C.\n2. $BF79 bit 7 marks EXOS as running: when it was clear, SP is kept at $BF7A and the stack\n   moves below $B217.\n3. Pushes the old $BF79, AF', the old USR_P2/USR_P3 and USR_P1 (all replaced by the caller's\n   pages), HL, IY and IX.\n4. An interrupt goes to IRQ_HANDLER. A call gets the caller's A back and, for function 16,\n   jumps to EXOS_VARIABLE with KRN_CALL_DONE as its return; other codes run KRN_DISPATCH,\n   after the\n   EI at $0056 (an EXOS call from interrupt code clears carry there instead and is refused).","comment":"Caller's page 2"},
        "1042": {"comment":"kept at $0054"},
        "1045": {"comment":"Page 2 = the system segment"},
        "1049": {"comment":"Caller's HL"},
        "1052": {"comment":"$BF79: EXOS running?"},
        "1058": {"comment":"It is now"},
        "1060": {"comment":"Nested: keep the stack"},
        "1062": {"comment":"Caller's SP"},
        "1066": {"comment":"EXOS stack below the pager"},
        "1069": {"synopsis":"Saves the old $BF79 with the nesting flags and carries the interrupt flag (carry) into AF'.","comment":"Old $BF79 and Z (nested?)"},
        "1070": {"comment":"Bit 7 off, carry kept"},
        "1073": {"comment":"Carry (interrupt?) to AF'"},
        "1074": {"comment":"Caller's AF'"},
        "1075": {"comment":"Old USR_P2/USR_P3"},
        "1079": {"comment":"Caller's page 2 and page 3"},
        "1082": {"comment":"become USR_P2/USR_P3"},
        "1085": {"comment":"Old USR_P1"},
        "1090": {"comment":"Caller's page 1 into USR_P1"},
        "1093": {"comment":"Caller's HL"},
        "1101": {"comment":"Exit ends in RET at $0057"},
        "1107": {"comment":"Interrupt?"},
        "1110": {"comment":"Caller's A to A'"},
        "1114": {"comment":"Function code"},
        "1117": {"comment":"EXOS 16 (EXOS variable)?"},
        "1122": {"comment":"Carry stays set unless in an interrupt"},
        "1123": {"comment":"$0056: EI (OR A in an interrupt)"},
        "1126": {"comment":"Run the function"},
        "1129": {"synopsis":"End of an EXOS call, with the status in A (also the return of function 16's handler,\nEXOS_VARIABLE):\nno interrupts while the exit is built."},
        "1132": {"synopsis":"Writes L = A (the status) and H to $0054/$0055; EXOS 0 (reset) comes here with H = $C9 (RET\nat $0055)."},
        "1133": {"synopsis":"Common exit: writes HL to $0054/$0055 (the LD A,n operand and the next opcode), pops IX, IY\nand the caller's HL ($BF7C), restores USR_P1 and page 1, swaps the caller's page 2/3 for the\nold USR_P2/P3 and restores AF'. A nested exit leaves through PAGER_EXIT with the current\nstack; the outermost one clears $BF79, moves a pending FLAG_SOFT_IRQ to $BFC7 and, when a\nsoftware interrupt routine is set ($003D), turns the RET at $0057 into a JR to it.","comment":"Status operand and next opcode"},
        "1144": {"comment":"USR_P1"},
        "1148": {"comment":"Page 1 = the caller's"},
        "1151": {"comment":"USR_P1 = the old value"},
        "1152": {"comment":"Caller's page 2/3 for"},
        "1156": {"comment":"the old USR_P2/USR_P3"},
        "1161": {"comment":"Caller's AF' back"},
        "1162": {"comment":"Old $BF79; Z = outermost"},
        "1163": {"comment":"Nested: leave on this stack"},
        "1165": {"comment":"$BF79 back (bit 7 clear)"},
        "1168": {"comment":"FLAG_SOFT_IRQ: one pending?"},
        "1174": {"comment":"To $BFC7, the code for the routine"},
        "1177": {"comment":"Clear FLAG_SOFT_IRQ"},
        "1182": {"comment":"Software interrupt address set?"},
        "1190": {"comment":"JR at $0057: on to $003C"},
        "1195": {"synopsis":"Outermost exit: back to the caller's stack.","comment":"Caller's SP"},
        "1199": {"synopsis":"Leaves through PAGER_EXIT (H = page 3, L = page 2).","comment":"Page 3, page 2 and out"},
        "1202": {"synopsis":"The interrupt handler (from KERNEL_ENTRY, both register sets saved).\n1. Writes OR A at $0056 (EXOS calls refused, no EI) and counts the interrupt at $BFEC.\n2. Picks the lowest latched source of port B4h (bits 1, 3, 5, 7) into D.\n3. Video: VIDEO_IRQ_PORTS. Then USER_ISR, if set, with D; at 1 Hz SEG1_CLOCK with A = 0.\n4. Calls the interrupt entry (+0) of each device in the chain (type 0) whose DD_IRQFLAG has\n   the source bit.\n5. Resets that latch (port B4h = $BFC5 OR D), writes EI at $0056 and leaves through KRN_EXIT\n   with POP AF at $0055."},
        "1208": {"comment":"$0056 = OR A: in an interrupt"},
        "1213": {"comment":"Count the interrupt"},
        "1217": {"comment":"Latched sources"},
        "1222": {"comment":"Keep the lowest one only"},
        "1225": {"comment":"D = the source bit"},
        "1226": {"comment":"Video (NICK) interrupt?"},
        "1231": {"comment":"USER_ISR set?"},
        "1237": {"comment":"Call it with D = the source"},
        "1242": {"comment":"1 Hz interrupt?"},
        "1247": {"comment":"Walk the device chain"},
        "1250": {"synopsis":"Loop: takes the next device (in page 1, segment in A); a device whose type byte is not 0 is\nskipped; one whose DD_IRQFLAG has the source bit gets its interrupt entry called.","comment":"Next device"},
        "1253": {"comment":"End of the chain?"},
        "1255": {"comment":"E = its segment"},
        "1256": {"comment":"Type byte not 0? Skip"},
        "1261": {"comment":"DD_IRQFLAG has the source?"},
        "1266": {"comment":"Entry +0: interrupt"},
        "1267": {"comment":"Stack check value for DEV_CALL_BC"},
        "1275": {"comment":"Page 1 = the device's segment"},
        "1280": {"synopsis":"All devices done: resets the serviced latch, re-enables interrupts at exit (EI at $0056),\nrestores both register sets and leaves through KRN_EXIT with POP AF at $0055.","comment":"Enabled sources"},
        "1283": {"comment":"Reset the serviced latch"},
        "1286": {"comment":"$0056 = EI"},
        "1297": {"comment":"$0055 = POP AF"},
        "1302": {"synopsis":"At each video interrupt: writes the border colour ($BFE0) to port 81h, FIXBIAS to port 80h\nfrom bits 3-7 of $BFE1 (bits 0-2 from $BFD3, 0-1 from $BFEB) shifted right 3, and bit 5\n(tape sound off) of PORTB5 from $BFD1, then port B5h.","comment":"$BFE0 / $BFE1"},
        "1306": {"comment":"Border colour"},
        "1308": {"comment":"Bits 0-2 from $BFD3, 3-7 from $BFE1"},
        "1315": {"comment":"Bits 0-1 from $BFEB"},
        "1322": {"comment":"Into position"},
        "1325": {"comment":"FIXBIAS"},
        "1327": {"comment":"Bit 5 from $BFD1"},
        "1330": {"comment":"PORTB5"},
        "1338": {"comment":"Kept, and to the port"},
        "1342": {"synopsis":"Calls entry A of the device at HL with the stack check value $5319, then goes on as\nDEV_CALL_BC (the LD BC hides DEV_CALL_BC's opcode in its operand: $053F/$0540 never run).","comment":"Stack check value; DEV_CALL_BC starts inside"},
        "1345": {"synopsis":"Calls entry A of a device.\nEntry: A = entry number (0 interrupt, 12 initialise ...); HL = the device's type byte in\npage 1 (its segment there); BC = minus the lowest allowed SP; E = the segment for page 1\nduring the call; D = the routine's A.\nExit: the routine's results; A = $FA (.NODEV) when the type byte is not 0, $FC (.STACK) when\nSP is too low.\nThe routine runs with its segment in page 3 through PAGER_CALL, IY = the descriptor + $4000,\nand $BF79 counted up while it runs.","comment":"Keep the entry number"},
        "1346": {"comment":"Type byte 0?"},
        "1348": {"comment":"No: error $FA (.NODEV)"},
        "1351": {"comment":"SP + BC: enough stack?"},
        "1354": {"comment":"No: error $FC (.STACK)"},
        "1360": {"comment":"IY = the descriptor, +$4000"},
        "1366": {"comment":"Keep page 1"},
        "1369": {"comment":"To DD_TAB"},
        "1376": {"comment":"Table's segment"},
        "1377": {"comment":"into page 1"},
        "1380": {"comment":"Entry * 2 into the table"},
        "1385": {"comment":"HL = the routine"},
        "1389": {"comment":"Page 1 for the call"},
        "1393": {"comment":"Routine's A"},
        "1397": {"comment":"Count the device call in $BF79"},
        "1402": {"comment":"Call it in its segment"},
        "1406": {"comment":"Count it back"},
        "1408": {"synopsis":"Runs EXOS function A (not 16).\nEntry: A = code; carry clear = called from interrupt code.\nCode 0 goes to EXOS_RESET (reset), 1-11 to EXOS_CHANNEL (the channel functions), 17-34\nthrough\nEXOS_FN_TABLE with A = 0; 12-15 and above 34 return $FF (.IFUNC), and any call from\ninterrupt code $FE (.ILLFN).","comment":"From interrupt code: refused"},
        "1410": {"comment":"EXOS 0 (reset)?"},
        "1414": {"comment":"Codes 1-11: channels"},
        "1419": {"comment":"Above 34?"},
        "1423": {"comment":"Below 17?"},
        "1427": {"comment":"Table entry (code - 17) * 2"},
        "1437": {"comment":"A = 0 on entry"},
        "1439": {"synopsis":"Returns error $FF (.IFUNC): no such function.","comment":"Error $FF (.IFUNC)"},
        "1442": {"synopsis":"Returns error $FE (.ILLFN): an EXOS call not allowed here (from interrupt code).","comment":"Error $FE (.ILLFN)"},
        "1445": {"synopsis":"Links the devices of ROM segment A.\nEntry: A = segment.\nThe ROM is put into page 1 and its device chain followed from ($4008). Each\npseudo-descriptor (its XX_SIZE bytes up to the size byte) is copied below ($BF93) in the\nsystem segment, when the copy and its XX_RAM bytes stay at or above $8020; it is linked at\nthe head of the device chain (LINK_DEVICE) and given the ROM's segment in DD_TAB. A device\nthat does not fit is skipped."},
        "1447": {"comment":"The ROM's device chain"},
        "1450": {"comment":"Keep the segment"},
        "1451": {"synopsis":"Loop: follows the chain to the next pseudo-descriptor (its size byte); 0 ends it."},
        "1455": {"comment":"End of the chain?"},
        "1457": {"comment":"XX_SIZE: bytes to copy"},
        "1461": {"comment":"Below the device area"},
        "1466": {"comment":"Copy the descriptor down"},
        "1468": {"comment":"XX_RAM"},
        "1473": {"comment":"Copy + device RAM"},
        "1474": {"comment":"Below $8020?"},
        "1480": {"comment":"Does not fit: skip it"},
        "1483": {"comment":"New bottom of the device area"},
        "1486": {"comment":"Page 1 = the system segment"},
        "1489": {"comment":"The copy's DD_TYPE"},
        "1491": {"comment":"in page-1 form"},
        "1495": {"comment":"Link it into the chain"},
        "1499": {"comment":"To DD_TAB's segment byte"},
        "1504": {"comment":"Back to the ROM in page 1"},
        "1506": {"comment":"The ROM's segment"},
        "1508": {"synopsis":"Back to XX_NEXT of this pseudo-descriptor and on to the next one."},
        "1509": {"comment":"XX_NEXT"},
        "1513": {"synopsis":"Links the device at DE (its DD_TYPE, page-1 form, segment A) at the head of the device chain\n($BFC0, through LINK_NODE) and copies its name (length first) to $BF36. A device with unit\ncount\n0 then replaces the older devices of the same name (SAME_NAME_LP).\nEntry: DE = the device; A = its segment.\nExit: HL = DE."},
        "1514": {"comment":"Chain head"},
        "1517": {"comment":"Link at the head"},
        "1520": {"comment":"To DD_UNIT_COUNT"},
        "1524": {"comment":"Unit count"},
        "1526": {"comment":"Copy the name to $BF36"},
        "1533": {"comment":"Unit count not 0: done"},
        "1535": {"synopsis":"Loop: looks for an older device named as at $BF36 (FIND_DEVICE); each one found has its type\nbyte\ndecremented (from 0, so the device is no longer used).","comment":"Name to find"},
        "1538": {"comment":"Another older device of the same name?"},
        "1541": {"comment":"None left"},
        "1542": {"comment":"DD_TYPE 0 -> $FF: it no longer answers"},
        "1543": {"comment":"Go on with the devices after it"},
        "1545": {"synopsis":"Names differ: restores the registers and goes on with the next node."},
        "1548": {"synopsis":"Searches a device chain for a device by name and unit. The links are 3 bytes (address low,\nhigh, segment) just below each node, and NEXT_LINK pages each node's segment into page 1. A\ndevice answers only while its DD_TYPE is 0; one with a unit count of n takes units 0 to n-1\nand passes higher units on with C reduced by n; a unit count of 0 takes any unit.\nEntry: HL = the node (or chain head) to go on after; DE = the name (length byte, then\nupper-case letters); C = the unit.\nExit: Z (A = 0) when the chain ends without a match; NZ with A = $FF when found, HL = IY =\nthe device's DD_TYPE in page 1, C = the unit within it.\nChanges: A, B, C, HL, IY, page 1.","comment":"Next node, paged into page 1"},
        "1551": {"comment":"End of chain: not found"},
        "1554": {"comment":"Name's length byte is at DD_TYPE + 7"},
        "1558": {"comment":"Compare length + 1 bytes"},
        "1561": {"synopsis":"Compares the name at DE with the node's, length byte first.","comment":"Same byte?"},
        "1565": {"comment":"Different: next device"},
        "1567": {"comment":"Whole name compared?"},
        "1569": {"comment":"Names match"},
        "1572": {"comment":"DD_TYPE still 0?"},
        "1573": {"comment":"Superseded: skip it"},
        "1575": {"comment":"IY = the descriptor"},
        "1578": {"comment":"A = minus the unit count"},
        "1581": {"comment":"Unit beyond this device's units?"},
        "1582": {"comment":"No (or any unit taken): found"},
        "1584": {"comment":"Unit counted on past this device"},
        "1587": {"synopsis":"Returns A = $FF and NZ: the device at HL takes unit C.","comment":"A = $FF, NZ"},
        "1590": {"synopsis":"Puts a node at the head of a chain. The head is the 3-byte link (address low, high, segment)\njust below HL; the old head moves into the 3 bytes below the new node, so the new node\npoints at what was first.\nEntry: HL = just past the chain's head link; A = the new node's segment, DE = its address\n(with that segment paged in).\nExit: HL = DE - 3, the new node's link.\nChanges: A, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"To the head's segment byte"},
        "1591": {"synopsis":"LINK_NODE entered with HL already on the head's segment byte (from $0FA8).","comment":"Old segment out, new in"},
        "1594": {"comment":"Old high byte out, new in"},
        "1597": {"comment":"Old low byte out, new in"},
        "1599": {"comment":"HL = the new node"},
        "1600": {"comment":"Its link = the old head: segment"},
        "1603": {"comment":"high byte"},
        "1605": {"comment":"low byte"},
        "1607": {"synopsis":"Follows a chain link: reads the 3 bytes below HL (address low, high, segment) and pages that\nsegment into page 1.\nEntry: HL = a node or a chain head (just past its link).\nExit: Z with A = 0 when the segment is 0 (end of chain; page 1 unchanged); otherwise NZ, A =\nthe segment, now in page 1, and HL = the next node.\nChanges: A, B, HL.","comment":"Segment byte"},
        "1609": {"comment":"High byte"},
        "1611": {"comment":"Low byte"},
        "1614": {"comment":"Segment 0: end of chain"},
        "1616": {"comment":"Page 1 = the node's segment"},
        "1619": {"synopsis":"EXOS 0 (reset): C = the reset flags. Clears RST_ADDR, the software interrupt address ($003D)\nand USER_ISR, puts a NOP over the EI at $0056, then:\n1. b7 set: cold start, jumps to the top of the ROM ($C000).\n2. b6 set: frees every user segment (FREE_ALL_USER).\n3. Any of b5-b0 set: goes through RESET_SYSTEM (devices re-initialised).\n4. Puts the EI back at $0056, clears the nesting flag $BF79 and leaves through the call exit\n   at KRN_STATUS_EXIT, dropping the return to KRN_CALL_DONE, with H = $C9 (RET) in place of\n   $B7 (OR A): the\n   return code in the null segment then returns before its OR A and EI, so the reset comes\n   back with interrupts disabled and the flags not matching A, as the specification says.","comment":"No interrupts while the system is rebuilt"},
        "1620": {"comment":"RST_ADDR = 0: next reset is cold"},
        "1626": {"comment":"No software interrupt routine"},
        "1629": {"comment":"No USER_ISR"},
        "1632": {"comment":"NOP at $0056: no EI on the way out"},
        "1636": {"comment":"Reset flags"},
        "1637": {"comment":"b7 into carry"},
        "1638": {"comment":"Cold start: run the ROM from the top"},
        "1641": {"comment":"b6 into carry; Z if b5-b0 clear"},
        "1643": {"comment":"b6: give back every user segment"},
        "1647": {"comment":"Any of b5-b0: re-initialise devices"},
        "1650": {"comment":"EI back at $0056"},
        "1655": {"comment":"Nesting flag $BF79 clear"},
        "1659": {"comment":"$0055 = RET: no OR A, no EI on return"},
        "1661": {"comment":"Drop the return to KRN_CALL_DONE"},
        "1662": {"comment":"Leave through the call exit"},
        "1665": {"synopsis":"EXOS 18 (redirect channel): sets carry and runs on into EXOS 17's code, which then stores\nthe redirect byte (16 below the channel's node) instead of the capture byte.","comment":"Carry: redirect, not capture"},
        "1666": {"synopsis":"EXOS 17 (capture channel), and EXOS 18 from EXOS_REDIRECT with carry set: A = the main\nchannel, C = the secondary channel ($FF cancels). Finds the main channel (FIND_CHANNEL,\nwhich takes\nthe number plus one) and stores C + 1 in its RAM: 15 bytes below the channel's node for a\ncapture (reads), 16 below for a redirect (writes); 0 there means none.\nExit: A = 0, or error $FB (.ICHAN) when the main channel is $FF or does not exist.","comment":"E = secondary channel"},
        "1667": {"comment":"Keep the carry: 17 or 18"},
        "1668": {"comment":"A = main channel"},
        "1669": {"comment":"Number + 1; channel $FF gives Z"},
        "1670": {"comment":"Find the main channel"},
        "1673": {"comment":"Error $FB (.ICHAN) if there is none"},
        "1675": {"comment":"Not found"},
        "1677": {"comment":"Capture byte is 15 below the node"},
        "1679": {"comment":"Carry (redirect): one byte lower"},
        "1682": {"comment":"Channel + 1; $FF becomes 0 (none)"},
        "1684": {"comment":"OK"},
        "1686": {"synopsis":"Drops the saved flags and returns error $FB (.ICHAN).","comment":"Drop the saved flags"},
        "1688": {"synopsis":"EXOS 21 (link device): DE = the descriptor's DD_TYPE in the caller's memory, BC = the device\nRAM wanted.\n1. Refuses a call from inside a device (error $FE, .ILLFN) and a descriptor whose DD_TYPE is\n   not 0 (error $F1, .IDESC).\n2. With BC not 0, gets the RAM through EXOS 27 with $BF84 = $F0, empties the channel cache,\n   and moves the bottom of the system segment's device area ($BF93) down by BC + 2; $BFB4\n   gets $BFBD minus the new bottom.\n3. LINK_DEV_CHAIN links the descriptor and calls its initialise entry.\nExit through CHAN_EXIT with A = 0; the alternate registers are kept.","comment":"Origin flags of the call"},
        "1689": {"comment":"Error $FE (.ILLFN) from a device"},
        "1692": {"comment":"Page the descriptor into page 1"},
        "1695": {"comment":"DD_TYPE must be 0"},
        "1697": {"comment":"Error $F1 (.IDESC)"},
        "1700": {"comment":"Device RAM wanted?"},
        "1702": {"comment":"None: link it"},
        "1706": {"comment":"Mark the request as device RAM"},
        "1711": {"comment":"DE = the size"},
        "1713": {"comment":"EXOS 27: allocate it"},
        "1715": {"comment":"Mark off again"},
        "1719": {"comment":"No room: return the error"},
        "1720": {"comment":"Empty the channel cache"},
        "1723": {"comment":"Device area bottom down by BC + 2"},
        "1730": {"comment":"New bottom"},
        "1735": {"comment":"Area size: $BFBD less the bottom"},
        "1743": {"synopsis":"Links the descriptor (in the segment now in page 1) at the head of the device chain through\nthe routine at LINK_DEVICE, which also retires older devices of the same name, then calls\nits\ninitialise entry (+24) with interrupts off and a NOP at $0056 in place of the EI.","comment":"Segment holding the descriptor"},
        "1747": {"comment":"No interrupts while the chain changes"},
        "1748": {"comment":"Link it at the head of the device chain"},
        "1752": {"comment":"Descriptor's segment back"},
        "1754": {"comment":"Keep the alternates as a channel call does"},
        "1758": {"comment":"NOP at $0056 during the init"},
        "1762": {"comment":"Entry 12: initialise (+24)"},
        "1764": {"comment":"Call the device"},
        "1767": {"comment":"EI back at $0056"},
        "1773": {"comment":"OK"},
        "1775": {"comment":"Leave through the channel-call exit"},
        "1778": {"synopsis":"EXOS 22 (read EXOS boundary): returns C = the shared segment ($BF9E, 0 if none), DE = the\nEXOS boundary ($BF91) and A = 0.","comment":"Shared segment"},
        "1782": {"comment":"EXOS boundary"},
        "1786": {"comment":"OK"},
        "1788": {"synopsis":"EXOS 23 (set user boundary): DE = the new user boundary. Error $F4 (.ISEG) without a shared\nsegment, $F3 (.IBOUND) above the EXOS boundary; otherwise stores it at $BF8F, A = 0. Also\ncalled by LOAD_NEW_APP.","comment":"Is a segment shared?"},
        "1792": {"comment":"Error $F4 (.ISEG)"},
        "1795": {"comment":"Above the EXOS boundary?"},
        "1800": {"comment":"Error $F3 (.IBOUND)"},
        "1803": {"comment":"OK: store it"},
        "1806": {"synopsis":"EXOS 24 (allocate segment). The RAM segments' numbers are bytes in a list in the system\nsegment, from ($BF9C) up: the page-0 segment, the user segments, the device segments, the\nfree segments (in ascending order), then from ($BF9A) the system's own, ending with $FF. The\ncounts are at $BF9F (free), $BFA0 (user), $BFA1 (device) and $BFA2 (system).\nEntry: F' = the call's origin flags (NZ: from inside a device).\n1. With a segment free it takes the lowest: for a user call it moves below the device block\n   into the user block, for a device it becomes the top device entry. Returns C = the\n   segment, DE = $4000, A = 0.\n2. Without one, a user call gets the lowest system segment as the shared segment ($BF9E): C\n   = it, DE = the EXOS boundary (also the new user boundary), A = $7F (the .SHARE warning).\n3. Error $F5 (.NOSEG) for a device, or when a segment is already shared.","comment":"Free segments?"},
        "1811": {"comment":"Yes: take one"},
        "1813": {"comment":"Origin flags"},
        "1814": {"comment":"A device gets no shared segment"},
        "1816": {"comment":"Lowest system segment"},
        "1820": {"comment":"Already shared?"},
        "1824": {"comment":"Then none left"},
        "1826": {"comment":"It becomes the shared segment"},
        "1828": {"comment":"Warning $7F (.SHARE)"},
        "1830": {"comment":"DE = EXOS boundary"},
        "1834": {"synopsis":"Stores DE as the user boundary ($BF8F) and returns.","comment":"User boundary = DE"},
        "1839": {"synopsis":"Takes a free segment for the caller.","comment":"B = free count"},
        "1840": {"comment":"One fewer free"},
        "1841": {"comment":"To the device count"},
        "1843": {"comment":"Origin flags"},
        "1844": {"comment":"A = device count"},
        "1845": {"comment":"To the user count"},
        "1846": {"comment":"User call: count it there"},
        "1848": {"comment":"Device: nothing to move past"},
        "1849": {"comment":"Device count"},
        "1850": {"synopsis":"Counts the segment for its new owner and finds the lowest free entry.","comment":"One more for this owner"},
        "1851": {"comment":"Free block ends below ($BF9A)"},
        "1854": {"synopsis":"Steps down B entries to the lowest free one.","comment":"Down to the lowest free entry"},
        "1857": {"synopsis":"Moves the segment at HL below the C entries under it (they shift up one byte); used with C =\nthe device count, and by RELEASE_SYS_SEG with C = free + device entries. Runs on into\nALLOC_DONE.","comment":"C = entries to move past"},
        "1859": {"comment":"A = the segment"},
        "1860": {"comment":"Nothing to move"},
        "1862": {"comment":"Shift those entries up one"},
        "1867": {"comment":"Segment goes below them"},
        "1868": {"synopsis":"Returns C = the segment, DE = $4000 and A = 0.","comment":"C = segment"},
        "1869": {"comment":"A whole segment: boundary $4000"},
        "1872": {"comment":"OK"},
        "1874": {"synopsis":"Returns error $F5 (.NOSEG).","comment":"Error $F5 (.NOSEG)"},
        "1877": {"synopsis":"Gives up the lowest system segment (the entry at ($BF9A)); RESET_SYSTEM calls it until it\nreturns\nZ, and $1182 calls it too.\n1. Segment $FF (the system segment itself) is never given: Z, A = $FF.\n2. The shared segment becomes a user segment: nothing shared, user boundary 0, returns Z\n   with A = 0.\n3. Any other goes into the free block in order (FREE_COUNT_UP), returning NZ.\nChanges: A, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Lowest system segment"},
        "1881": {"comment":"The system segment itself?"},
        "1883": {"comment":"Yes: nothing to give"},
        "1884": {"comment":"System block starts one higher"},
        "1889": {"comment":"One fewer system segment"},
        "1893": {"comment":"Is it the shared segment?"},
        "1896": {"comment":"No: make it free"},
        "1898": {"comment":"Nothing shared any more"},
        "1901": {"comment":"A = free count"},
        "1903": {"comment":"One more user segment"},
        "1905": {"comment":"Entries to move past: free + device"},
        "1906": {"comment":"HL = new ($BF9A)"},
        "1907": {"comment":"User boundary 0"},
        "1910": {"comment":"Back to the segment's entry"},
        "1911": {"comment":"B = 0"},
        "1912": {"comment":"Move it into the user block"},
        "1914": {"synopsis":"Adds segment A to the free block, which ends just below HL (= ($BF9A)), keeping the block in\nascending order; FREE_COUNT_UP is the same with that address in DE.\nChanges: A, B, D, HL; one more free segment counted.","comment":"Block's end into DE"},
        "1915": {"synopsis":"Counts one more free segment, then sorts A into the free block from its top down.","comment":"One more free segment"},
        "1919": {"comment":"B = entries to sort through"},
        "1921": {"comment":"Store A on top, then sort"},
        "1923": {"synopsis":"Moves A down while the entry below it is bigger.","comment":"Entry below"},
        "1925": {"comment":"Not bigger: A is in place"},
        "1926": {"comment":"Bigger: move it up"},
        "1929": {"synopsis":"Steps down one entry and stores A there.","comment":"Down one entry"},
        "1930": {"synopsis":"Stores A at HL and goes on sorting while B lasts; $1024 enters here.","comment":"A here for now"},
        "1931": {"comment":"Every free entry passed?"},
        "1934": {"synopsis":"EXOS 25 (free segment): C = the segment.\nEntry: F' = the call's origin flags (Z: a user call).\n1. The shared segment just stops being shared (it stays the system's; user boundary 0).\n2. Otherwise the segment is looked for in the caller's block (the user segments after the\n   page-0 segment, or the device segments for a device), taken out, and sorted into the free\n   block; the owner's count drops by one. Returns A = 0.\n3. Error $F4 (.ISEG) when the segment is not the caller's, or C = 0 with nothing shared.","comment":"The shared segment?"},
        "1939": {"comment":"No: look in the caller's block"},
        "1941": {"comment":"C = 0 and nothing shared"},
        "1944": {"comment":"Nothing shared any more"},
        "1946": {"synopsis":"Sets the user boundary to 0.","comment":"User boundary 0"},
        "1951": {"synopsis":"Finds the segment in the caller's block and takes it out.","comment":"A' = the segment, A = origin flags"},
        "1952": {"comment":"Keep the origin"},
        "1954": {"comment":"Free count"},
        "1956": {"comment":"User count"},
        "1958": {"comment":"Device count"},
        "1961": {"comment":"List start: the page-0 segment"},
        "1964": {"comment":"Skip it"},
        "1965": {"comment":"User call: search the user block"},
        "1967": {"comment":"Device: skip the user block"},
        "1968": {"comment":"Search the device block"},
        "1969": {"comment":"Only the free block above it"},
        "1970": {"synopsis":"Searches the owner's C entries from HL for the segment; A' gets the number of entries above\nthe block that move down with the gap.","comment":"Entries above the block"},
        "1971": {"comment":"A = the segment"},
        "1972": {"comment":"Empty block?"},
        "1976": {"comment":"Look for it"},
        "1978": {"comment":"Not the caller's"},
        "1980": {"comment":"Entries after it up to the top"},
        "1983": {"comment":"None"},
        "1985": {"comment":"Close the gap"},
        "1990": {"synopsis":"Sorts the segment into the free block and lowers its owner's count.","comment":"A = the segment"},
        "1991": {"comment":"Into the free block"},
        "1994": {"comment":"User count"},
        "1997": {"comment":"Origin"},
        "1998": {"comment":"User call"},
        "2000": {"comment":"Device count"},
        "2001": {"synopsis":"Lowers the owner's count and returns A = 0.","comment":"One fewer"},
        "2002": {"comment":"OK"},
        "2004": {"synopsis":"Drops the saved origin and returns error $F4 (.ISEG).","comment":"Drop the origin"},
        "2005": {"synopsis":"Returns error $F4 (.ISEG).","comment":"Error $F4 (.ISEG)"},
        "2008": {"synopsis":"Frees every user segment: EXOS 25 as a user call for each segment number 0-255 (and the\nshared segment stops being shared). Used by the reset (flag b6) and before a new application\nloads.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"From segment 0"},
        "2010": {"synopsis":"Frees segment C if the user has it, then the next."},
        "2011": {"comment":"As a user call (Z)"},
        "2013": {"comment":"EXOS 25"},
        "2017": {"comment":"Up to 255"},
        "2021": {"synopsis":"EXOS 19 (set default device): DE = the device name (no colon, a unit after '-'), C = the\ndevice type, copied to DEF_TYPE (EXOS variable 3, $BFC8). The name is parsed by\nPARSE_DEV_NAME; the\nunit goes to $BF18 and, if a name is given, the name (length byte, upper-case letters) to\n$BF19, so a unit alone keeps the old name. Error $FD (.INAME) for an empty or bad name; A =\n0 otherwise.","comment":"Page the caller's string in"},
        "2024": {"synopsis":"EXOS_SET_DEFDEV with DE already addressable: the start-up code ($02B0) sets the first\ndefault device this way from the ROM's text at TAPE_NAME.","comment":"Name's length"},
        "2026": {"comment":"Keep the type"},
        "2027": {"comment":"Parse name and unit into $BF36"},
        "2030": {"comment":"E = the type"},
        "2031": {"comment":"Empty or bad: error $FD (.INAME)"},
        "2033": {"comment":"DEF_TYPE (EXOS variable 3)"},
        "2037": {"comment":"Default unit at $BF18"},
        "2042": {"comment":"Name given?"},
        "2044": {"comment":"No: keep the old name"},
        "2045": {"comment":"Length byte + letters"},
        "2047": {"comment":"To $BF19"},
        "2048": {"synopsis":"Copies C bytes from HL to DE (B = 0) and returns A = 0."},
        "2049": {"comment":"B = 0: copy C bytes"},
        "2053": {"synopsis":"Returns error $FD (.INAME).","comment":"Error $FD (.INAME)"},
        "2056": {"synopsis":"EXOS 20 (read system status): copies the 7 bytes from $BF9E to the caller's block at DE: the\nshared segment, then the numbers of free, user, device, system, working and faulty segments.\nReturns B = $21 (version 2.1), A = 0, DE unchanged.","comment":"Keep DE"},
        "2057": {"comment":"Page the block in"},
        "2060": {"comment":"Shared segment, then the counts"},
        "2065": {"comment":"Copy them"},
        "2068": {"comment":"Version 2.1"},
        "2070": {"synopsis":"Restores DE and returns; EXOS 26 jumps here with error $FC (.STACK), its saved SP going into\nDE.","comment":"DE back"},
        "2072": {"synopsis":"EXOS 26 (scan system extensions): DE = the command string (length byte, then text).\n1. The first call since start-up ($BF78 = 0) offers action 1 (cold start) to the extensions\n   instead.\n2. Otherwise copies the string onto the system stack (error $FC, .STACK, when there is no\n   room), skipping leading spaces and upper-casing the first word; $BF8B holds the copy's\n   address.\n3. If that word is HELP it is dropped and the rest copied the same way for action 3 (help\n   string); otherwise the action is 2 (command string).\n4. CALL_EXTENSIONS gets C = the action, B = the first word's length, DE = the copy; then SP\n   is restored.","comment":"First scan since start-up?"},
        "2077": {"comment":"Later scans find 1"},
        "2080": {"comment":"First: offer the cold start"},
        "2083": {"comment":"Page the string in"},
        "2086": {"comment":"Keep SP"},
        "2091": {"comment":"String length"},
        "2095": {"comment":"Room for it on the stack?"},
        "2101": {"comment":"Error $FC (.STACK)"},
        "2103": {"comment":"No room"},
        "2105": {"comment":"HL = SP - length - 1"},
        "2109": {"comment":"Stack moves below the copy"},
        "2112": {"comment":"Copy's address"},
        "2115": {"comment":"B = length"},
        "2117": {"comment":"Action 2 (command string)"},
        "2119": {"comment":"Hides LD A,3 at $0848: action 3 (help)"},
        "2122": {"comment":"A' = action"},
        "2123": {"comment":"Pre-count for the loop"},
        "2124": {"synopsis":"Skips leading spaces, counting B down.","comment":"String used up?"},
        "2127": {"comment":"Next character"},
        "2129": {"comment":"Space?"},
        "2133": {"comment":"Back before the first non-space"},
        "2134": {"synopsis":"Copies the rest of the string to the copy: the first word upper-cased (C = its length), the\nremainder as it is.","comment":"Length byte"},
        "2139": {"comment":"C counts the first word"},
        "2141": {"synopsis":"Copies the first word, upper-casing a-z.","comment":"One more letter"},
        "2143": {"comment":"End of string"},
        "2148": {"comment":"Lower case?"},
        "2156": {"comment":"To upper case"},
        "2158": {"synopsis":"Stores a character of the first word; a space ends the word.","comment":"Store it"},
        "2159": {"comment":"End of the first word?"},
        "2163": {"synopsis":"Copies the rest unchanged.","comment":"End of string?"},
        "2168": {"comment":"Next character as it is"},
        "2172": {"synopsis":"Checks whether the first word is HELP.","comment":"First word four letters long?"},
        "2177": {"comment":"Compare with HELP_TEXT"},
        "2185": {"synopsis":"Compares the first word with HELP_TEXT letter by letter.","comment":"Next letter"},
        "2189": {"comment":"Not HELP"},
        "2193": {"comment":"HELP: drop it from the string"},
        "2200": {"comment":"Action 3, copy the rest again"},
        "2202": {"synopsis":"Offers the string to the extensions and puts the stack back.","comment":"B = first word's length"},
        "2203": {"comment":"C = action"},
        "2205": {"comment":"DE = the copy"},
        "2209": {"comment":"Offer it to the extensions"},
        "2212": {"comment":"Stack back above the copy"},
        "2215": {"synopsis":"The word HELP (4 bytes, no end mark), compared with the first word of an EXOS 26 string by\nSCAN_HELP_CMP, which addresses it as $C8A6 + 1."},
        "2219": {"synopsis":"Offers an action to the system extensions until one takes it (returns C = 0).\n1. The extensions loaded into RAM, from the chain whose head is at $BFC0-$BFC2: each is\n   called at its node's address with bit 15 set, with its segment in page 3.\n2. Then the ROMs, from a list of 4-byte entries going down from ($BF9C) - 3: the ROM's\n   segment (0 ends the list), the segment for page 1, then IY high and low (the ROM's RAM);\n   a segment of $FF is skipped, the others are called at $C00A.\nEntry: C = the action code; B, DE = its parameters.\nExit: C = 0 if an extension took it, else unchanged; the alternate BC, DE, HL are kept.","comment":"Keep the alternate registers"},
        "2224": {"comment":"Chain of extensions in RAM"},
        "2227": {"synopsis":"Calls the next RAM extension unless the action has been taken.","comment":"Taken (C = 0)?"},
        "2232": {"comment":"Next extension, paged in"},
        "2236": {"comment":"End of chain: the ROMs"},
        "2239": {"comment":"Its entry as seen in page 3"},
        "2241": {"comment":"Call it with its segment in page 3"},
        "2247": {"synopsis":"Starts on the list of ROM extensions below the segment list.","comment":"Below the segment list"},
        "2254": {"synopsis":"Calls the next ROM extension unless the action has been taken.","comment":"Taken?"},
        "2259": {"comment":"List pointer"},
        "2260": {"comment":"ROM's segment"},
        "2263": {"comment":"0: end of list"},
        "2266": {"comment":"Page 1 = its RAM segment"},
        "2271": {"comment":"IY = its RAM pointer"},
        "2280": {"comment":"Segment $FF: skip"},
        "2282": {"comment":"The ROM's entry point"},
        "2285": {"comment":"Call it with its segment in page 3"},
        "2290": {"synopsis":"Drops the list pointer, then EXT_DONE.","comment":"Drop the list pointer"},
        "2291": {"synopsis":"Restores the alternate BC, DE, HL and returns."},
        "2292": {"synopsis":"EXT_DONE entered with the alternate set already selected.","comment":"Alternate registers back"},
        "2297": {"synopsis":"EXOS 16 (EXOS variable): B = 0 read, 1 write, 2 toggle; C = the variable; D = the value to\nwrite. Reached by a jump from the call entry ($045F), so it pushes the call exit\nKRN_CALL_DONE as\nits return.\n1. Variables 0 and 2-39 are the bytes $BFC5 + number; variable 1 (FLAG_SOFT_IRQ) is $BFF2.\n   Returns D = the variable's value, A = 0.\n2. Any access to 36 (REM1) or 37 (REM2) also calls segment 1's routine whose address is at\n   $BFC3, with A = 0 or 1 and D = the value.\n3. Numbers from 40 go to the extensions as action 4 (unknown EXOS variable) with E = the\n   number; error $F2 (.IVAR) if none takes it.","comment":"Return through the call exit"},
        "2301": {"comment":"Number 40 or more?"},
        "2304": {"comment":"Not kept here: ask the extensions"},
        "2307": {"comment":"Variable 1: FLAG_SOFT_IRQ"},
        "2315": {"comment":"Others at $BFC5 + number"},
        "2318": {"synopsis":"Reads, writes or toggles the variable at HL.","comment":"B = 0: read"},
        "2322": {"comment":"B = 1: write"},
        "2324": {"comment":"Toggle: complement it"},
        "2327": {"synopsis":"Stores D in the variable.","comment":"Store the new value"},
        "2328": {"synopsis":"Returns the variable in D; 36 and 37 also go to the remote routine.","comment":"D = the value"},
        "2330": {"comment":"Variable 36 (REM1)?"},
        "2334": {"comment":"Variable 37 (REM2)?"},
        "2336": {"synopsis":"Calls VAR_SET_REMOTE for variables 36 and 37, then returns A = 0.","comment":"Update the remote switch"},
        "2339": {"comment":"OK"},
        "2341": {"synopsis":"Calls segment 1's routine at the address in $BFC3 (set by segment 1) with A = 0 for REM1 or\n1 for REM2 and D = the value; it sets the matching bit of port B5h.","comment":"A' = which remote"},
        "2342": {"comment":"Segment 1"},
        "2344": {"comment":"Its remote routine"},
        "2347": {"comment":"Call it in page 3"},
        "2350": {"synopsis":"Offers a variable from 40 up to the extensions (action 4).","comment":"E = the number"},
        "2351": {"comment":"Action 4: unknown EXOS variable"},
        "2356": {"comment":"Taken?"},
        "2359": {"comment":"Error $F2 (.IVAR)"},
        "2362": {"synopsis":"EXOS 28 (explain error code): A = the code, DE = a 64-byte buffer. Offers action 5 to the\nextensions with B = the code; the one that takes it leaves B = a segment and DE = an address\nthere of the message (length byte, then text). The message is copied to the buffer with that\nsegment in page 0 (the buffer in page 1). Returns A = 0, DE unchanged.","comment":"Keep the buffer address"},
        "2363": {"comment":"A = the error code"},
        "2365": {"comment":"Action 5: explain error code"},
        "2370": {"comment":"HL = the message"},
        "2373": {"comment":"Page the buffer in"},
        "2376": {"comment":"No interrupts with page 0 changed"},
        "2377": {"comment":"Page 0 = the message's segment"},
        "2380": {"comment":"Message address in page 0"},
        "2384": {"comment":"Length byte + text"},
        "2388": {"comment":"Copy it"},
        "2390": {"comment":"Page 0 back (USR_P0)"},
        "2397": {"comment":"OK"},
        "2399": {"synopsis":"EXOS 29 (load module): A = the channel (kept at $BF80), DE = a 16-byte buffer. Reads the\nmodule header into $BF08-$BF17 and acts on its type byte ($BF09):\n1. First byte not 0, or type 0: error $EF (.ASCII) with B = the first byte; end of file at\n   the first byte: error $EC (.NOMOD).\n2. Type 10 (end of file module): error $EC (.NOMOD).\n3. Types 6 and 7 (system extensions): LOAD_SYS_EXT.\n4. Type 5 (new application): LOAD_NEW_APP.\n5. Others go to the extensions as action 6 (B = channel, DE = the header); if none takes\n   them the header goes to the caller's buffer and the call returns error $EE (.ITYPE), B =\n   the type.\nDE is unchanged.","comment":"Keep the buffer address"},
        "2400": {"comment":"A = channel"},
        "2401": {"comment":"Channel kept at $BF80"},
        "2407": {"comment":"EXOS 5: first header byte"},
        "2409": {"comment":"Read failed"},
        "2411": {"comment":"Not 0: not a module"},
        "2414": {"comment":"Header copy at $BF08"},
        "2421": {"comment":"EXOS 5: type byte"},
        "2423": {"comment":"Read failed"},
        "2428": {"comment":"Type at $BF09"},
        "2430": {"comment":"Type 0: not a module"},
        "2433": {"comment":"14 more header bytes"},
        "2439": {"comment":"To $BF0A"},
        "2440": {"comment":"EXOS 6: the rest of the header"},
        "2442": {"comment":"B = type"},
        "2445": {"comment":"Type 6: absolute system extension?"},
        "2450": {"comment":"Type 7: relocatable extension?"},
        "2454": {"comment":"Type 5: new application?"},
        "2459": {"comment":"Type 10: end of file"},
        "2463": {"comment":"Others: ask the extensions"},
        "2466": {"comment":"Action 6: load module"},
        "2471": {"comment":"B = channel"},
        "2475": {"comment":"H = type"},
        "2476": {"comment":"Taken?"},
        "2483": {"comment":"Page the caller's buffer in"},
        "2486": {"comment":"Give the header back"},
        "2494": {"comment":"B = type"},
        "2495": {"comment":"Error $EE (.ITYPE)"},
        "2497": {"comment":"Hides LD A,$EF at $09C2: error .ASCII"},
        "2500": {"comment":"Hides LD A,$EC at $09C5: error .NOMOD"},
        "2503": {"synopsis":"Restores DE and returns; EXOS 30 returns through here too.","comment":"DE back"},
        "2505": {"synopsis":"The first byte could not be read: end of file becomes $EC (.NOMOD), other errors return as\nthey are.","comment":"End of file?"},
        "2507": {"comment":"Then error $EC (.NOMOD)"},
        "2511": {"synopsis":"Loads a system extension (types 6 and 7): gets a block of the module's length + 3 in a\nsystem-extension segment through ALLOC_DEV_RAM (from the bottom of the free space for type\n6, the\ntop for type 7), loads the code after a 3-byte link (EXOS 6 for type 6, segment 1's\nrelocating loader for type 7) and records the new bottom ($4001) or top ($4003) in the\nsegment's header. Then LOAD_EXT_LINK; a load error goes to LOAD_EXT_FAIL.","comment":"0 absolute, 1 relocatable"},
        "2514": {"comment":"Module length from the header"},
        "2519": {"comment":"Room for the link too"},
        "2522": {"comment":"Block in a system-extension segment"},
        "2526": {"comment":"No room"},
        "2529": {"comment":"Keep the block's address"},
        "2530": {"comment":"Code goes after the 3-byte link"},
        "2535": {"comment":"IX = the segment's flags ($4005)"},
        "2539": {"comment":"Type 7: relocatable"},
        "2544": {"comment":"EXOS 6: load it"},
        "2546": {"comment":"Drop the block address"},
        "2547": {"comment":"Load failed"},
        "2549": {"comment":"New bottom of the free space"},
        "2553": {"comment":"Flag: absolute code here"},
        "2559": {"synopsis":"Has segment 1 load and relocate a type 7 module at DE.","comment":"Keep the code address"},
        "2560": {"comment":"Relocate for page 3, where it runs"},
        "2562": {"comment":"Segment 1, service 5"},
        "2568": {"comment":"Failed"},
        "2570": {"comment":"New top of the free space"},
        "2574": {"comment":"Flag: relocatable code here"},
        "2578": {"synopsis":"Links the loaded extension (its code is its node) at the head of the RAM extension chain and\ncalls it with action 8 (initialise). Returns A = 0.","comment":"DE = the code, its node"},
        "2580": {"comment":"Its segment"},
        "2582": {"comment":"RAM extension chain"},
        "2588": {"comment":"Entry as seen in page 3"},
        "2593": {"comment":"Action 8: initialise"},
        "2595": {"comment":"Call it with its segment in page 3"},
        "2598": {"comment":"OK"},
        "2601": {"synopsis":"After a failed load: if the segment's flags ($4005) show nothing loaded in it yet, frees it\nas a device segment (EXOS 25 with NZ origin) and cuts it off the end of the chain of\nsystem-extension segments ($BF99, each segment's byte $4000 naming the next).","comment":"Keep the error"},
        "2602": {"comment":"Anything loaded in this segment?"},
        "2606": {"comment":"Yes: keep it"},
        "2608": {"comment":"Free it as a device segment (NZ)"},
        "2610": {"comment":"The segment"},
        "2614": {"comment":"EXOS 25"},
        "2618": {"comment":"Chain head"},
        "2621": {"comment":"Hides LD HL,$4000 at $0A3E: the next link"},
        "2625": {"comment":"Page the chained segment in"},
        "2627": {"comment":"Does it point at ours?"},
        "2629": {"comment":"No: follow the chain"},
        "2631": {"comment":"Cut ours off the end"},
        "2633": {"synopsis":"Returns the error with DE restored.","comment":"The error"},
        "2636": {"synopsis":"No block to be had: drops the type and returns the error with DE restored.","comment":"Drop the type"},
        "2639": {"synopsis":"Loads a new application (module type 5) at $0100 and starts it.\n1. Works out how many 16K pages $0100 up to the program's end needs. Error $F7 (.NORAM) if\n   the end passes $BFFF, if the free and user segments plus two (the page-0 one and a shared\n   one) are too few, or, when the last page has to be the shared segment, if the end is\n   above the EXOS boundary.\n2. Clears RST_ADDR and the software interrupt address, frees every user segment, calls\n   ARRANGE_VID_SEGS, allocates a segment for each further page into USR_P1 on, and sets the\n   user\n   boundary.\n3. Reads the program a page at a time (EXOS 6) with each page mapped into page 1.\n4. Sets RST_ADDR = $0100 and SP = $B217 and goes on in the start-up code at $013B with C =\n   $20, the reset flags for a new application. A read error goes to APP_LOAD_ERROR.","comment":"Length"},
        "2643": {"comment":"Last byte's offset"},
        "2644": {"comment":"Fits from $0100 below $C000?"},
        "2649": {"comment":"No"},
        "2651": {"comment":"Count pages"},
        "2653": {"comment":"High byte of the last address"},
        "2655": {"synopsis":"Counts the 16K pages up to the program's last byte.","comment":"One more page"},
        "2658": {"comment":"Until the last byte's page"},
        "2660": {"comment":"D = last byte's high byte in its page - $40"},
        "2661": {"comment":"Free segments"},
        "2666": {"comment":"+ user segments"},
        "2667": {"comment":"+ page 0 and a shared one"},
        "2669": {"comment":"Minus the pages needed"},
        "2670": {"synopsis":"Errors when segments are too few; with exactly enough the last page is the shared segment,\nso the program's end must be below the EXOS boundary.","comment":"Too few"},
        "2672": {"comment":"Some to spare"},
        "2674": {"comment":"End offset in the last page"},
        "2679": {"comment":"Below the EXOS boundary?"},
        "2684": {"comment":"No"},
        "2686": {"synopsis":"Gives up the old program's memory and maps the pages for the new one.","comment":"RST_ADDR = 0"},
        "2692": {"comment":"No software interrupt routine"},
        "2695": {"comment":"Keep the page count"},
        "2696": {"comment":"Free every user segment"},
        "2699": {"comment":"Then ARRANGE_VID_SEGS"},
        "2703": {"comment":"Page registers from USR_P0"},
        "2706": {"synopsis":"Allocates a segment for each page after page 0, into USR_P1 on.","comment":"All pages have segments?"},
        "2709": {"comment":"As a user call"},
        "2712": {"comment":"EXOS 24"},
        "2717": {"comment":"Next page register"},
        "2718": {"comment":"Its segment"},
        "2721": {"synopsis":"Sets the user boundary and starts reading the program at $0100.","comment":"User boundary at the end (if shared)"},
        "2724": {"comment":"Length"},
        "2728": {"comment":"Load address $0100"},
        "2731": {"comment":"Room in page 0 above $0100"},
        "2734": {"synopsis":"Reads the next chunk: the rest of the program or the rest of the page, whichever is less.","comment":"Keep the address"},
        "2735": {"comment":"Its page into page 1"},
        "2738": {"comment":"A = 0: last chunk"},
        "2739": {"comment":"Rest fits in this page?"},
        "2743": {"comment":"No: read to the page's end"},
        "2747": {"comment":"A not 0: more to come"},
        "2748": {"comment":"Left after this chunk"},
        "2750": {"synopsis":"Reads a chunk of BC bytes to DE.","comment":"Keep the flag and the size"},
        "2755": {"comment":"EXOS 6: read the chunk"},
        "2757": {"comment":"Load error"},
        "2759": {"comment":"DE = chunk size"},
        "2761": {"comment":"BC = what is left"},
        "2764": {"comment":"Next load address"},
        "2766": {"comment":"A full page of room from now on"},
        "2769": {"comment":"More to read?"},
        "2772": {"comment":"Warm reset restarts at $0100"},
        "2778": {"comment":"Stack just below the pager at $B217"},
        "2781": {"comment":"Reset flags $20: a new application"},
        "2783": {"comment":"Into the start-up code"},
        "2786": {"synopsis":"Returns error $F7 (.NORAM) with DE restored.","comment":"Error $F7 (.NORAM)"},
        "2790": {"synopsis":"The application failed to load after the old program's memory was given up: writes\nLOAD_ERR_TEXT to channel $FF (the default channel), waits, resets with flags $60 (user\nsegments freed, devices relinked) and calls EXOS 26 with $BF78 = 0, so the extensions are\noffered the cold start (action 1) again. Nothing follows the EXOS 26 call but the text.","comment":"The message"},
        "2793": {"comment":"36 bytes"},
        "2796": {"comment":"Default channel"},
        "2798": {"comment":"EXOS 8: write it"},
        "2800": {"comment":"Wait 65536 rounds"},
        "2803": {"synopsis":"Delay loop.","comment":"Waste time"},
        "2808": {"comment":"Until HL = 0"},
        "2810": {"comment":"Free user segments, relink devices"},
        "2812": {"comment":"EXOS 0: reset"},
        "2814": {"comment":"Fresh stack"},
        "2818": {"comment":"Next scan is a cold start"},
        "2822": {"comment":"EXOS 26: offer action 1 again"},
        "2824": {"synopsis":"CR LF, \"LOAD Error.  Re-starting system.\", CR LF: 36 bytes written by APP_LOAD_ERROR with\nEXOS 8."},
        "2860": {"synopsis":"EXOS 30 (load relocatable module): A = the channel (kept at $BF80), DE = the load address.\nPages DE's segment into page 1 and has segment 1 load and relocate the module (service 5);\nreturns through LOAD_RET, DE unchanged.","comment":"Keep DE"},
        "2861": {"comment":"A = channel"},
        "2862": {"comment":"Channel kept at $BF80"},
        "2865": {"comment":"Return through LOAD_RET"},
        "2870": {"comment":"Page DE's segment into page 1"},
        "2874": {"synopsis":"Asks segment 1 for service 5, load and relocate a module (five INC A from 0); LOAD_RELOC_EXT\ncalls it.","comment":"Service number counts up from 0"},
        "2875": {"comment":"Service 5: load relocatable"},
        "2876": {"synopsis":"Asks segment 1 for service 4 (A = 0 on entry), the one-second tick: segment 1 adds 1 to\nSECOND_COUNTER, counts TIMER down (software interrupt $40 at 0) and advances the clock. The\n1 Hz interrupt paths at $04DC and $2D81 call it.","comment":"Service 4: the 1 Hz tick"},
        "2877": {"synopsis":"EXOS 33 (set date): the table enters the row of INC A here, at $0B3D-$0B40 one byte apart,\nwith A = 0, so each EXOS code gets its own service number of segment 1: 33 -> 3. C = year, D\n= month, E = day (BCD).","comment":"Service 3: set date (EXOS 33)"},
        "2878": {"synopsis":"EXOS 34 (read date): segment 1 service 2; returns C = year, D = month, E = day (BCD), which\nsegment 1 keeps at $BF75-$BF77.","comment":"Service 2: read date (EXOS 34)"},
        "2879": {"synopsis":"EXOS 31 (set time): segment 1 service 1; C = hours, D = minutes, E = seconds (BCD).","comment":"Service 1: set time (EXOS 31)"},
        "2880": {"synopsis":"EXOS 32 (read time): segment 1 service 0; returns the time (C, D, E) that segment 1 keeps at\n$BF72-$BF74. Every entry above ends here: calls segment 1's service entry at $C00D through\nthe pager at $B217 with A = the service number, then segment 0 is paged back. The start-up\ncode jumps here with A = 6 ($0162).","comment":"A' = service number"},
        "2881": {"comment":"Segment 1"},
        "2883": {"comment":"Its service entry"},
        "2886": {"comment":"Call it in page 3"},
        "2889": {"synopsis":"EXOS 1-11 (channel calls): A = the function code; the caller's A (the channel) is in A'.\n1. Codes 1-4 are refused from inside a device (error $FE, .ILLFN) and empty the channel\n   cache. Channel $FF means the default channel (DEF_CHAN, $BFC9); error $FB (.ICHAN) if\n   that is $FF too.\n2. Codes 1 and 2 (open, create) go to CHAN_OPEN.\n3. Others find the channel in a cache of three 4-byte entries at $BFA5 (channel + 1, node\n   address, segment; 0 ends it), else in the channel chain (FIND_CHANNEL), and that find\n   goes to\n   the cache's front. Error $FB (.ICHAN) if there is none.\n4. Reads (5, 6, 9) follow the channel's capture byte, writes (7, 8) its redirect byte\n   (CHAN_REDIRECT).\n5. DEV_CALL_CHAN calls the device's entry for the code; an error from a secondary channel\n   cancels\n   the capture or redirect that led to it.\nThe alternate BC, DE, HL are kept; the exit is CHAN_EXIT.","comment":"Keep the alternate registers"},
        "2893": {"comment":"Codes 1-4 give carry"},
        "2896": {"comment":"Channel cache"},
        "2899": {"comment":"Codes 5-11"},
        "2901": {"comment":"Origin flags; A = channel"},
        "2902": {"comment":"From a device: error $FE (.ILLFN)"},
        "2905": {"comment":"Channels change: empty the cache"},
        "2908": {"synopsis":"Takes the channel number; $FF means the default channel.","comment":"A = channel"},
        "2909": {"comment":"Channel $FF?"},
        "2912": {"comment":"DEF_CHAN (EXOS variable 4)"},
        "2915": {"comment":"Also $FF?"},
        "2918": {"synopsis":"Opens a channel (codes 1, 2) or looks it up in the cache.","comment":"D = channel + 1"},
        "2919": {"comment":"A = code"},
        "2920": {"comment":"Codes 1, 2: open or create"},
        "2925": {"comment":"4-byte cache entries"},
        "2928": {"synopsis":"Looks for channel D in the cache.","comment":"Entry's channel"},
        "2929": {"comment":"0: end of cache"},
        "2932": {"comment":"This channel?"},
        "2933": {"comment":"Next entry (flags kept)"},
        "2937": {"comment":"Node from the entry, paged in"},
        "2940": {"comment":"Go on"},
        "2942": {"synopsis":"Not cached: searches the channel chain and puts the channel at the cache's front.","comment":"Channel + 1"},
        "2943": {"comment":"Search the chain"},
        "2946": {"synopsis":"Error $FB (.ICHAN) when the channel was not found, or channel $FF has no default.","comment":"No such channel"},
        "2948": {"comment":"Keep the node"},
        "2949": {"comment":"Shift two entries back; the third drops"},
        "2959": {"comment":"Front entry"},
        "2960": {"comment":"Channel + 1"},
        "2962": {"comment":"Node address"},
        "2968": {"comment":"Segment"},
        "2970": {"comment":"HL = node"},
        "2971": {"synopsis":"Follows the capture byte (reads) or the redirect byte (writes) of the channel at HL to the\nsecondary channel, then that one's, up to 256 links (B); the loop re-enters at $0B9E, the\nPOP DE hidden in CP $D1. Too many links, or a secondary channel that is gone, clears the\nbyte.","comment":"Link counter"},
        "2973": {"comment":"Hides POP DE at $0B9E: the loop's re-entry"},
        "2975": {"comment":"Function code"},
        "2976": {"comment":"Codes 3, 4: neither"},
        "2978": {"comment":"Code 9: capture"},
        "2982": {"comment":"10, 11: neither"},
        "2984": {"comment":"5, 6 capture; 7, 8 redirect (carry)"},
        "2987": {"synopsis":"Reads the capture (15 below the node) or redirect (16 below) byte; 0 means none.","comment":"Keep the node"},
        "2988": {"comment":"Byte 15 or 16 below it"},
        "2995": {"comment":"Keep the code"},
        "2996": {"comment":"Secondary channel + 1"},
        "2998": {"comment":"None"},
        "3000": {"comment":"C = this channel's segment"},
        "3004": {"comment":"Byte's address into the other HL"},
        "3010": {"comment":"Find the secondary channel"},
        "3014": {"comment":"Gone"},
        "3016": {"comment":"Found: check its own links"},
        "3018": {"comment":"Too many links: cancel"},
        "3019": {"synopsis":"Clears the capture or redirect byte: its channel is gone, or the links loop.","comment":"Cancel the capture or redirect"},
        "3021": {"synopsis":"No secondary channel: carries on with this one.","comment":"Function code"},
        "3022": {"synopsis":"Calls the channel's device and handles an error from a secondary channel.","comment":"Keep the last link's segment"},
        "3024": {"comment":"Keep the last link byte's address"},
        "3027": {"comment":"Call the device's entry for the code"},
        "3031": {"comment":"Carry: go on at CLOSE_FREE_BUF"},
        "3034": {"comment":"Last link's segment"},
        "3035": {"comment":"A = status"},
        "3036": {"comment":"Not an error ($00-$7F)?"},
        "3040": {"comment":"Error: link's channel back in page 1"},
        "3043": {"comment":"Cancel its capture or redirect"},
        "3045": {"comment":"Return the error"},
        "3047": {"synopsis":"Error exits of the channel calls: one LD A,n each, all but the first hidden in LD HL,nn.\nCHAN_ERRORS $FE (.ILLFN), $0BEA $FB (.ICHAN), $0BED $F9 (.CHANX), $0BF0 $FA (.NODEV), $0BF3\n$FD\n(.INAME). Each drops the saved function code and leaves through CHAN_EXIT.","comment":"Error $FE (.ILLFN)"},
        "3049": {"comment":"Hides LD A,$FB at $0BEA: .ICHAN"},
        "3052": {"comment":"Hides LD A,$F9 at $0BED: .CHANX"},
        "3055": {"comment":"Hides LD A,$FA at $0BF0: .NODEV"},
        "3058": {"comment":"Hides LD A,$FD at $0BF3: .INAME"},
        "3061": {"comment":"Drop the function code"},
        "3062": {"synopsis":"Exit of the channel calls (and of EXOS 21): keeps A in A', restores the alternate BC, DE, HL\nand returns with A.","comment":"Keep the status"},
        "3063": {"synopsis":"CHAN_EXIT entered with the status already in A'.","comment":"Alternate registers back"},
        "3067": {"comment":"A = status"},
        "3069": {"synopsis":"EXOS 1 and 2 (open, create channel): error $F9 (.CHANX) if the channel exists. Takes the\ndevice name before a colon in the caller's string, or the default device ($BF19, unit $BF18)\nwhen there is none, parses it (error $FD, .INAME), finds the device with FIND_DEVICE (error\n$FA, .NODEV) and calls its open or create entry; the code runs on past $0C00.","comment":"A = channel + 1"},
        "3070": {"comment":"Caller's registers: DE = the name"},
        "3071": {"comment":"Channel already open?"},
        "3074": {"comment":"Channel already open? Error $F9 (.CHANX)"},
        "3076": {"comment":"Page the caller's name string into page 1"},
        "3079": {"comment":"Length of the name"},
        "3081": {"comment":"Empty name: to $0C15 (inside the next instruction)"},
        "3083": {"comment":"C = characters to search"},
        "3086": {"comment":"Past the length byte"},
        "3087": {"comment":"Look for a colon"},
        "3091": {"comment":"B = characters left after the colon"},
        "3092": {"comment":"Byte $0C15 is INC A: the empty-name path enters there with A = 0"},
        "3094": {"comment":"Unit = the default device's unit at $BF18"},
        "3098": {"comment":"A = whole name length (no device part)"},
        "3099": {"comment":"No colon (or empty name): default device"},
        "3101": {"comment":"HL = the colon"},
        "3104": {"comment":"A = length before the colon"},
        "3106": {"comment":"Parse the device name and unit into $BF36"},
        "3109": {"comment":"D = characters after the colon"},
        "3110": {"comment":"A = file name length"},
        "3111": {"comment":"DE = the colon, the file name's length position"},
        "3112": {"comment":"Bad device name: error $FD (.INAME)"},
        "3114": {"comment":"Device name empty (only a unit or nothing)?"},
        "3118": {"synopsis":"Open/create without a device name: the default device name kept at $BF19 (length, then the\nletters; set by EXOS 19) is used, with the unit in C.","comment":"Default device name"},
        "3121": {"synopsis":"Open/create, the device known: copies the file name (A characters after DE) to $BF54, finds\nthe device named at HL (unit in C) in the device chain, records the new channel's device in\n$BF84-$BF88 and calls the device's open or create entry. A device that returns without\nhaving allocated its channel buffer gets error $F8 (.NOBUF)."},
        "3123": {"comment":"Copy and check the file name"},
        "3128": {"comment":"Bad character: error $FD (.INAME)"},
        "3130": {"comment":"Name too long: error $FD (.INAME)"},
        "3132": {"comment":"DE = device name"},
        "3133": {"comment":"Head of the device chain"},
        "3136": {"comment":"Find the device by name and unit"},
        "3139": {"comment":"Not found: error $FA (.NODEV)"},
        "3141": {"comment":"Keep the descriptor's address"},
        "3142": {"comment":"To DD_FLAGS"},
        "3145": {"comment":"Bit 0: a video device"},
        "3150": {"comment":"Back to the caller's registers: D = channel key"},
        "3151": {"comment":"E = video flag"},
        "3152": {"comment":"$BF84 = video flag, $BF85 = channel key"},
        "3156": {"comment":"$BF89 = $FF: waiting for EXOS 27"},
        "3161": {"comment":"Descriptor address"},
        "3162": {"comment":"$BF86/$BF87 = the device's descriptor"},
        "3165": {"comment":"Segment it lies in"},
        "3167": {"comment":"$BF88 = the descriptor's segment"},
        "3170": {"comment":"A = function code: entry 1 open or 2 create"},
        "3171": {"comment":"Call the device"},
        "3175": {"comment":"Device failed?"},
        "3176": {"comment":"Yes: return its status"},
        "3179": {"comment":"Keep the status"},
        "3180": {"comment":"Still $FF if no buffer was allocated"},
        "3183": {"comment":"Error $F8 (.NOBUF)"},
        "3185": {"comment":"No buffer?"},
        "3186": {"comment":"Yes: return .NOBUF"},
        "3189": {"comment":"Restore $BF89"},
        "3190": {"comment":"Return the device's status"},
        "3193": {"synopsis":"Calls a channel's device: the channel descriptor is the 16 bytes below HL (the channel\npointer, in page 1 with its segment paged), the device's descriptor pointer is in it (HL-14\nto HL-12) and the key byte at HL-5 says whether the channel is real; with a zero key the\ndevice is not called. The device gets the entry number in A (the EXOS function code; 13 =\nbuffer moved) through DEV_CALL. Used by the channel calls ($0BD3) and after a buffer move\n($0F99, $1151).\nEntry: A = device entry number, HL = channel pointer, $BF81/$BF83 = the list link before the\nchannel (from CHAN_LIST_STEP).\nExit: A' = the device's status, IY = the channel pointer, IX/B = $BF81/$BF83 as they were at\nentry, A and F as at entry.","comment":"Keep the entry number and flags"},
        "3195": {"comment":"Link before this channel"},
        "3199": {"comment":"and its segment"},
        "3204": {"comment":"Channel pointer"},
        "3205": {"comment":"E = segment of the channel RAM"},
        "3208": {"comment":"16 bytes back: the channel descriptor"},
        "3215": {"comment":"D = the channel's key byte (pointer-5)"},
        "3219": {"comment":"Zero key: no device to call"},
        "3222": {"comment":"To pointer-11: past the device link"},
        "3226": {"comment":"Follow the link to the device's descriptor"},
        "3230": {"comment":"A = entry number"},
        "3231": {"comment":"Call the device's entry"},
        "3234": {"comment":"Keep its status in A'"},
        "3237": {"synopsis":"Common exit: pops the saved registers into IY, B and IX.","comment":"IY = channel pointer"},
        "3239": {"comment":"B = the saved link's segment"},
        "3240": {"comment":"IX = the saved link's address"},
        "3242": {"comment":"Entry number and flags back"},
        "3244": {"synopsis":"Parses a device name of A characters starting at DE+1: letters (made upper case) go to $BF37\non with their count at $BF36, at most 28. A non-letter starts the unit number: an optional\n'-', then decimal digits. Also used by EXOS 19 (set default device, $07EB).\nEntry: A = length (not 0), DE = the byte before the name.\nExit: NZ = valid, C = unit number (0 if none); Z = bad name (29 letters, a '-' at the end or\na non-digit in the unit).","comment":"Device name buffer (length byte first)"},
        "3247": {"comment":"B = characters to read"},
        "3248": {"comment":"C = letters stored"},
        "3251": {"synopsis":"Takes the next character of the device name."},
        "3252": {"comment":"Next character"},
        "3253": {"comment":"Lower case?"},
        "3257": {"comment":"Make it upper case"},
        "3259": {"synopsis":"Is the (upper-cased) character a letter?","comment":"Below 'A': the unit starts"},
        "3263": {"comment":"Past 'Z'?"},
        "3265": {"comment":"Carry = not a letter"},
        "3268": {"comment":"Store the letter"},
        "3271": {"comment":"29 letters: too long"},
        "3276": {"comment":"Until the name ends"},
        "3278": {"synopsis":"The letters end: stores their count, then reads the unit number if the name goes on."},
        "3279": {"comment":"Store the name's length"},
        "3280": {"comment":"Unit 0"},
        "3282": {"comment":"Name ended (or too long, Z): return"},
        "3283": {"comment":"B = characters left, this one included"},
        "3284": {"comment":"A '-' before the unit?"},
        "3288": {"comment":"'-' was the last character?"},
        "3289": {"comment":"Yes: Z, bad name"},
        "3290": {"comment":"Skip the '-'"},
        "3291": {"synopsis":"Starts the unit number at 0.","comment":"Unit = 0"},
        "3292": {"synopsis":"Unit loop: returns the unit in A and C when no characters are left."},
        "3293": {"comment":"More digits?"},
        "3295": {"comment":"A = unit"},
        "3296": {"comment":"NZ: valid"},
        "3298": {"synopsis":"Adds the next decimal digit to the unit.","comment":"Unit times 10"},
        "3304": {"comment":"Next character"},
        "3306": {"comment":"Below '0'?"},
        "3310": {"comment":"Past '9'?"},
        "3314": {"comment":"Add the digit"},
        "3317": {"synopsis":"A character that is not a digit: returns Z (bad name).","comment":"Z: bad unit"},
        "3319": {"synopsis":"Copies the file name of an open/create, A characters after DE, to $BF55 on with its length\nat $BF54, making letters upper case. Allowed are '#' to '*', '-' to '9', '?', 'A' to 'Z' and\n'[' to '`' (and lower-case letters); at most 28.\nExit: NC NZ = valid (an empty name returns A = 1); C = a character that is not allowed; Z =\ntoo long.","comment":"File name buffer"},
        "3322": {"comment":"Its length"},
        "3323": {"comment":"No file name?"},
        "3326": {"comment":"NZ, NC: valid"},
        "3328": {"synopsis":"Copies a non-empty file name.","comment":"B = characters to copy"},
        "3332": {"synopsis":"Takes the next character of the file name."},
        "3333": {"comment":"Next character"},
        "3334": {"comment":"Below '#': not allowed"},
        "3338": {"comment":"'#' to '*' allowed"},
        "3342": {"comment":"'+' and ',' not allowed"},
        "3346": {"comment":"'-' to '9' allowed"},
        "3350": {"comment":"'?' allowed"},
        "3354": {"comment":"Up to 'Z': check for a letter"},
        "3358": {"comment":"'[' to '`' allowed"},
        "3362": {"comment":"Make a lower-case letter upper case"},
        "3364": {"synopsis":"Accepts only 'A' to 'Z' here.","comment":"Below 'A': not allowed"},
        "3368": {"comment":"Past 'Z'?"},
        "3370": {"comment":"Carry = not allowed"},
        "3373": {"synopsis":"Stores an allowed character.","comment":"Store the character"},
        "3376": {"comment":"29 characters: too long"},
        "3381": {"comment":"Until the name ends"},
        "3383": {"synopsis":"Stores the name's length and returns with the flags of the last test."},
        "3384": {"comment":"Store the length"},
        "3386": {"synopsis":"Looks for the channel with key A in the channel list. The list starts at the link\n$BFBA-$BFBC (seen through page 1 at $7FBD with the system segment paged there); each\nchannel's link to the next lies just below its pointer (pointer-3 to pointer-1: address low,\nhigh, segment) and its key byte at pointer-5. A link with segment 0 ends the list. Also used\nby EXOS 17/18 ($0686) and the channel calls.\nEntry: A = key.\nExit: NZ = found: HL = the channel pointer (page 1), A = its segment, paged into page 1; Z =\nnot found. $BF81/$BF83 hold the link that led to the last node reached (address, segment).","comment":"Compare the key byte 5 below each pointer"},
        "3388": {"synopsis":"Walks the channel list from its head for key A, comparing the byte C below each pointer;\nwith C = 0 it returns the first node.","comment":"List head at $BFBD, seen through page 1"},
        "3391": {"comment":"Keep the key"},
        "3392": {"comment":"System segment"},
        "3394": {"comment":"Page 1 = system segment"},
        "3397": {"synopsis":"Steps from the link at HL to the next node: saves the link in $BF81/$BF83 and follows it.\nWith C = 0 it returns after this one step; otherwise it goes on until the byte C below a\npointer is the key in A'. Exit as FIND_CHANNEL."},
        "3398": {"comment":"Remember the link's address"},
        "3401": {"comment":"and its segment"},
        "3404": {"comment":"Follow the link, paging the next node into page 1"},
        "3407": {"comment":"B = 0 for the subtraction"},
        "3409": {"comment":"End of the list: Z"},
        "3410": {"comment":"C = 0: return after one step"},
        "3413": {"comment":"A = key"},
        "3414": {"comment":"To the node's key byte"},
        "3416": {"comment":"The key wanted?"},
        "3417": {"comment":"Back to the pointer"},
        "3418": {"comment":"No: next node"},
        "3420": {"comment":"A = the node's segment, NZ"},
        "3422": {"synopsis":"EXOS 27 (allocate channel buffer): gives the channel being opened its RAM and descriptor,\nor, called by EXOS 21 (link device, $06B1, with $BF84 = $F0), a device its RAM. Allowed only\nwhile an open waits for its buffer ($BF89 = $FF), else error $FE (.ILLFN).\nEntry: DE = bytes that must lie in one segment; BC = more bytes that need not (video devices\nonly; ignored for others).\nExit: A = status ($F8 .NOBUF for more than 16K in one segment, $F7 .NORAM, $F6 .NOVID); IX =\nthe new channel RAM (the descriptor at IX+0 to IX+15, the RAM below IX); page 1 (USR_P1) =\nits segment.\n1. Works out the block's size: the request plus a 16-byte descriptor (2 bytes for device\n   RAM).\n2. Lets ARRANGE_VID_SEGS arrange the video segments.\n3. Walks the channel list for a gap below a channel big enough for the block\n   (ABUF_SEARCH_LP), or\n   reaches the end of the list (the EXOS boundary, $BF91).\n4. Checks, node by node down the list, that each block that has to move keeps its\n   one-segment part inside one segment (ABUF_SEG_LOOP); at the boundary takes free segments\n   for the\n   rest (ABUF_NEED_SEGS), then checks a video block against video RAM (ABUF_CHECK_VID).\n5. Moves the blocks below the insertion point down, calling each moved channel's device with\n   entry 13, buffer moved (ABUF_MOVE_LOOP).\n6. Links the new block into the list and fills its descriptor (ABUF_BUILD), then clears the\n   RAM\n   and returns IX (ABUF_FINISH).","comment":"Header size for device RAM"},
        "3425": {"comment":"Video flag of the device being opened"},
        "3428": {"comment":"Video device?"},
        "3432": {"comment":"Not video: no multi-segment part"},
        "3434": {"comment":"$BF84 = $F0: device RAM from EXOS 21"},
        "3436": {"synopsis":"A channel buffer: checks that an open waits for it and sets the 16-byte header.","comment":"Is an open waiting for its buffer?"},
        "3440": {"comment":"No: error $FE (.ILLFN)"},
        "3443": {"comment":"Header size for a channel: its descriptor"},
        "3445": {"synopsis":"Saves the caller's alternate registers and the stack pointer ($ABD1, for the error exit at\nABUF_ERROR), checks the size and starts the search.","comment":"Keep the caller's alternate set"},
        "3449": {"comment":"Back to the parameters"},
        "3450": {"comment":"Stack to unwind to on an error"},
        "3454": {"comment":"Keep the one-segment size"},
        "3455": {"comment":"Add the header"},
        "3456": {"comment":"Past 64K?"},
        "3458": {"comment":"16K or more?"},
        "3461": {"synopsis":"Size test of the block: past 64K or 16K or more in one segment goes to error $F8 (.NOBUF) at\n$0E98.","comment":"Too big: error $F8 (.NOBUF)"},
        "3464": {"comment":"DE = one-segment size with header"},
        "3466": {"comment":"Add the multi-segment part"},
        "3467": {"comment":"Past 64K: .NOBUF"},
        "3469": {"comment":"Total size"},
        "3470": {"comment":"One-segment size"},
        "3472": {"comment":"DE = total, HL = one-segment size"},
        "3474": {"comment":"Arrange the video segments"},
        "3477": {"comment":"Video flag doubled: Z plain, C device RAM"},
        "3482": {"comment":"C = 0: just the first node (video, device RAM)"},
        "3486": {"comment":"Plain channel: look for a node with key 0"},
        "3488": {"synopsis":"Finds the first node to try: for a plain channel the first node with key 0, for a video\nchannel the first node; device RAM goes straight below the list head.","comment":"Key 0"},
        "3489": {"comment":"Search the channel list"},
        "3492": {"comment":"Keep the node's segment and flags"},
        "3493": {"comment":"Flags of the kind of block"},
        "3494": {"comment":"Device RAM: place it at the head"},
        "3496": {"synopsis":"Search loop: on the first node a plain channel skips the key test; from then on a node that\nis not a channel (key 0) ends the search there. Otherwise the gap below the node is compared\nwith the block's size.","comment":"IX = this node"},
        "3499": {"comment":"Plain channel: try the gap here"},
        "3501": {"comment":"Key byte of the node"},
        "3505": {"comment":"Not a channel: place the block here"},
        "3507": {"synopsis":"Compares the free gap below the node (word at pointer-11) with the block's total size, then\nsteps to the next node.","comment":"Use the size registers"},
        "3508": {"comment":"Free gap below this node"},
        "3514": {"comment":"Gap smaller than the block? (carry)"},
        "3517": {"comment":"Keep the answer in D bit 0"},
        "3519": {"comment":"One step only"},
        "3521": {"comment":"To the next node"},
        "3524": {"comment":"End of the list?"},
        "3525": {"comment":"Yes: the block goes at the EXOS boundary"},
        "3527": {"comment":"Gap big enough?"},
        "3529": {"comment":"Yes: place the block below this node"},
        "3531": {"comment":"Flags back; NZ from here on (H is never 0)"},
        "3533": {"comment":"Try the next node"},
        "3535": {"synopsis":"The end of the list: the block's place is found from the EXOS boundary ($BF91).","comment":"EXOS boundary"},
        "3538": {"synopsis":"The block goes below the node whose link is in $BF81/$BF83 (paged in, IX): works out where\nits top and bottom fall from that node's gap (word at IX-11), resets $BF8B and starts the\nsegment check with IY on EXOS's segment list ($BF9A).","comment":"IX = the node the block goes below"},
        "3542": {"comment":"Page it into page 1"},
        "3547": {"comment":"Z (B is 0 here): the first node"},
        "3549": {"comment":"Keep the flags"},
        "3550": {"comment":"Its free gap"},
        "3556": {"comment":"Add the gap"},
        "3557": {"comment":"Swap with the saved total size"},
        "3567": {"comment":"No video address yet"},
        "3570": {"comment":"$BF8B = 0"},
        "3574": {"comment":"IY = EXOS's segment list pointer"},
        "3578": {"comment":"Borrow at $0DE7: to ABUF_NO_ROOM"},
        "3580": {"synopsis":"Checks one block's one-segment part (BC) against the room left in its segment (HL, the\naddress within the segment).","comment":"Address within the segment"},
        "3584": {"comment":"One-segment part fits?"},
        "3586": {"comment":"Yes"},
        "3588": {"comment":"No: push it to the next segment"},
        "3595": {"comment":"Past 64K: error $F8 (.NOBUF)"},
        "3599": {"comment":"Error $F7 (.NORAM)"},
        "3603": {"synopsis":"The block fits in its segment: for a video channel records the new bottom in $BF8B.","comment":"A video channel?"},
        "3609": {"comment":"$BF8B = its new bottom"},
        "3613": {"synopsis":"Saves DE, the flags and the node; after the first node, a node whose gap (IX-11) is at least\nDE ends the moves (ABUF_GAP_ENDS), otherwise DE is reduced by the gap."},
        "3614": {"comment":"Flags: Z for the first node"},
        "3618": {"comment":"First node: no gap test"},
        "3620": {"comment":"This node's gap"},
        "3626": {"comment":"DE zero?"},
        "3630": {"comment":"Gap at least DE?"},
        "3632": {"comment":"Yes: no further node moves"},
        "3634": {"comment":"Undo the subtraction"},
        "3635": {"synopsis":"Takes the node's gap off what is still owed.","comment":"DE less the gap"},
        "3640": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next node below; at the end of the list the boundary must move\n(ABUF_NEED_SEGS).","comment":"The node"},
        "3643": {"comment":"Nothing moved yet for it"},
        "3647": {"comment":"Follow its link"},
        "3650": {"comment":"End of the list"},
        "3652": {"comment":"IX = next node"},
        "3658": {"comment":"Keep the flags (NZ: not the first)"},
        "3666": {"comment":"Yes: error $F7 (.NORAM)"},
        "3669": {"synopsis":"Takes the next node's one-segment size (word at pointer-7) for the segment check.","comment":"BC = the node's one-segment size"},
        "3675": {"comment":"Check it at its new place"},
        "3677": {"synopsis":"Reached when the subtraction at $0DE7 borrows (not run in the profile): saves the state as\nABUF_NEXT_NODE does, sets HL = 0 - DE and goes to the video check with the EXOS boundary and\nno new\nsegments."},
        "3682": {"comment":"HL = 0 - DE"},
        "3688": {"synopsis":"A node's gap is at least DE: pushes what is left of it, keeps the EXOS boundary ($BF91) and\ntakes no segments."},
        "3689": {"comment":"EXOS boundary"},
        "3692": {"comment":"No new segments"},
        "3695": {"synopsis":"At the end of the list: takes the room between the user boundary ($BF8F) and the EXOS\nboundary ($BF91) off what is owed, then counts the 16K segments still needed (A), stepping\nIY down EXOS's segment list for each; with a shared segment ($BF9E not 0) none may be taken.\nRunning out gives error $F7 (.NORAM).","comment":"EXOS boundary"},
        "3698": {"comment":"User boundary"},
        "3702": {"comment":"Room in the shared segment"},
        "3704": {"comment":"Is there a shared segment?"},
        "3708": {"comment":"Free segments"},
        "3713": {"comment":"Shared segment: none to take"},
        "3714": {"synopsis":"B = the number of segments that may be taken, plus one (none with a shared segment).","comment":"Segments that may be tried, plus one"},
        "3716": {"comment":"HL = owed"},
        "3718": {"comment":"A = segments taken"},
        "3719": {"synopsis":"Counts the 16K segments needed for what is still owed.","comment":"Still owed after this segment?"},
        "3721": {"comment":"16K per segment"},
        "3724": {"comment":"No: done"},
        "3726": {"comment":"Take one more segment"},
        "3729": {"comment":"While there are free ones"},
        "3733": {"synopsis":"Error exits: ABUF_NORAM returns $F7 (.NORAM); $0E98, inside the next instruction, returns\n$F8\n(.NOBUF); ABUF_ERROR returns the status in A, resetting the stack to $ABD1.","comment":"Error $F7 (.NORAM)"},
        "3735": {"comment":"Byte $0E98 is LD A,$F8: error $F8 (.NOBUF) enters there"},
        "3738": {"synopsis":"Returns the error in A: resets SP from $ABD1 and leaves through the channel calls' exit\n(CHAN_EXIT), which restores the caller's alternate registers.","comment":"Drop everything pushed since $0D7A"},
        "3742": {"comment":"Caller's registers back via the exit"},
        "3746": {"synopsis":"The room suffices: computes the new EXOS boundary from what is left over and the user\nboundary ($BF8F)."},
        "3750": {"comment":"User boundary"},
        "3755": {"synopsis":"For a video block or device RAM ($BF84 not 0) checks video RAM: $BF8D less $BF8B must not go\nnegative, nor drop below the video address of segment $BF8A (its number's low two bits times\n16K); else error $F6 (.NOVID). $BF8D then becomes the difference.","comment":"DE = new EXOS boundary"},
        "3756": {"comment":"Keep the segment count"},
        "3757": {"comment":"Plain channel?"},
        "3761": {"comment":"Yes: no video RAM check"},
        "3763": {"comment":"$BF8D"},
        "3766": {"comment":"less $BF8B"},
        "3772": {"comment":"Below it: .NOVID"},
        "3774": {"comment":"Lowest video segment of the top run"},
        "3777": {"comment":"Its number times 16K: video address"},
        "3784": {"comment":"Room left inside video RAM?"},
        "3786": {"synopsis":"Error $F6 (.NOVID) if the check failed; otherwise stores the difference in $BF8D.","comment":"Error $F6 (.NOVID)"},
        "3788": {"comment":"No room: return it"},
        "3791": {"comment":"$BF8D = $BF8D less $BF8B"},
        "3794": {"synopsis":"Records the new EXOS boundary and segment list pointer and moves the segments taken from the\nfree count ($BF9F) to EXOS's count ($BFA2).","comment":"New EXOS boundary"},
        "3798": {"comment":"New segment list pointer"},
        "3802": {"comment":"A = segments taken"},
        "3803": {"comment":"Segments EXOS holds"},
        "3807": {"comment":"Add the new ones"},
        "3809": {"comment":"Free segments"},
        "3812": {"comment":"Take them off"},
        "3814": {"comment":"Carry: the first pass"},
        "3815": {"synopsis":"Move loop, once per node the check saved on the stack (the lowest first, the node the block\ngoes below last): pages the node in, writes its new gap (IX-11) and, except on the first\npass, its new link (IX-3 to IX-1), finds its segment in EXOS's segment list, copies the\nblock down (ABUF_COPY_LOOP) and calls its device with entry 13 (buffer moved, BC = the\ndistance). The\nlast pass builds the new block (ABUF_BUILD).","comment":"Flags of this pass"},
        "3817": {"comment":"IX = the node"},
        "3819": {"comment":"Its segment"},
        "3820": {"comment":"Page it into page 1"},
        "3822": {"comment":"NZ: not the node the block goes below"},
        "3831": {"synopsis":"Writes the node's new gap and, except on the first pass, its new link.","comment":"New gap below the node"},
        "3837": {"comment":"First pass?"},
        "3840": {"comment":"Link to the next node: segment"},
        "3843": {"comment":"address low"},
        "3846": {"comment":"address high"},
        "3849": {"synopsis":"Finds the node's segment in EXOS's segment list (from $ABD0 down) and sets up the source\n(page 0) and destination (page 1) of the copy."},
        "3852": {"comment":"Find the segment in EXOS's list"},
        "3855": {"synopsis":"Scans EXOS's segment list down for the segment in A.","comment":"Search down from $ABD0"},
